/* Chapter 7 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":7,"title":"Loan and Debt in Islamic Commercial Law","part":"part-ii","partRoman":"II","pages":[155,176],"minutes":55,"estimates":{"f1":55,"f2":65},"difficulty":"Intermediate","difficultyAlt":"intermediate","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch7-1","text":"Define Qard, Salaf, Dayn and ‘Ariyah and distinguish them.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-2","text":"Explain why any stipulated benefit on a loan is Riba and how this applies to banking.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-3","text":"State the Qur’anic guidance on documenting credit transactions (2:282–283).","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-4","text":"Describe the duties of debtors and creditors, Husnal Qadha and the treatment of current accounts.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-5","text":"Explain the rules on prepayment rebates, penalties on default and insolvency.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-6","text":"Explain Hawalah, Kafalah and Rihn, including risk, reward and benefits of a pledge.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-7","text":"Explain the prohibition of Bai‘ al Dayn and indexation of loans/debts.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-8","text":"Distinguish Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah, and explain why only the principal is repayable on a loan.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-9","text":"Explain why commercial interest is illegal under Shari'ah and how Islamic banks' deposit and financing structures relate to Qard and Dayn.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-10","text":"Explain the Shari'ah treatment of time value of money in loans/debts versus in trade.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-11","text":"State the Shari'ah instructions given to debtors and creditors, including Husnal Qadha and the rules on remitting part of a debt or granting a prepayment rebate.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-12","text":"Explain the Shari'ah-compliant approach to penalties on default, liquidated damages, and insolvency (Muflis).","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-13","text":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch7-14","text":"Explain why Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) is prohibited and why indexing loans/debts to inflation is not permitted.","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Every Murabaha, Ijarah or Salam creates a debt. The rules in this chapter govern what an Islamic bank may do when that debt is paid early, paid late, assigned, secured or eroded by inflation — the operational issues that separate Islamic banking from interest-based lending.","whyExpanded":"Because Islamic banks' asset-side financing overwhelmingly creates debt (through Murabaha, Ijarah, Salam, Istisna'a and similar trade/lease modes), the rules in this chapter -- on what a loan may and may not carry, how defaults and insolvency are handled, how debt can (and cannot) be assigned or secured, and why debt cannot be sold or indexed -- directly govern the legal boundaries of everyday Islamic banking operations. Understanding these rules is essential to distinguishing legitimate debt-recovery and risk-management tools from disguised Riba.","overview":"The chapter defines loans and debts, reaffirms the illegality of commercial interest, and sets out Qur’anic and Prophetic guidance on lending: what can be lent, repayment of principal only, no time value in debts, duties of debtors and creditors, gracious repayment, rebates, penalties and insolvency, Hawalah, Kafalah and pledges, sale of debt and inflation.","summary":"Islamic banks create debt through sale and lease contracts and take some current deposits as loans, so the rules on loans and debts are crucial. Only the principal of a loan or debt is payable; any addition is Riba, and no time value can be assigned once a receivable is stipulated. Gracious extra repayment without precondition is commendable but cannot be a system. Debtors must repay; creditors are urged to be lenient; security may be taken. Penalty clauses paid to charity are approved, with compensation for actual (not opportunity) loss through courts or arbitration. Contractual prepayment rebates are not allowed, though AAOIFI allows discretionary rebates. Debts may be assigned by Hawalah with recourse; sale of debt is prohibited. A bank-pledgee may benefit in return for maintenance with any excess going to the pledger. Debts are repaid without indexation, though lending in gold or a stable currency is allowed.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 7 sets out the Shari'ah law of loan and debt, distinguishing Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah, and establishing the central rule that a loan must be repaid at exactly its principal, since any stipulated benefit above it -- in quantity or quality -- is Riba. It shows how this rule shapes both conventional and Islamic banking structures (deposits and current accounts as Qard), explains why time value of money applies in trade but not in loans, and details the Shari'ah's instructions to debtors and creditors, including the narrow, discretion-only scope of Husnal Qadha and the technical rules on remitting part of an already-due debt. It then covers how default is handled today (charity-bound penalty clauses plus actual-loss compensation for Dayn, never for Qard) and how insolvency is resolved. Finally, it works through the mechanisms of Hawalah (assignment of debt, preserving recourse) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee and pledge), and closes with two firm prohibitions: selling debt at other than par value (Bai' al-Dayn) and indexing loans/debts to inflation, currencies, or commodities.","summarySection":"7.18","takeaways":["Qard, Salaf, Dayn, ‘Ariyah — know the differences.","Any stipulated benefit on a loan is Riba.","Current accounts: loans, no profit, service charges allowed.","No contractual rebate; discretionary rebate (AAOIFI).","Penalty to charity; compensation only for actual loss; none on Qard al Hasan.","Hawalah with recourse; sale of debt prohibited.","Pledger bears pledge risk; pledgee benefit only for maintenance.","No indexation; settlement-day currency switch at that day’s rate.","Qard (demand loan, similar item repaid) and Dayn (any credit-transaction debt) must be repaid at exactly the principal; 'Ariyah requires returning the exact borrowed item.","Any loan condition creating a benefit above principal -- in quantity or quality -- is void and constitutes Riba; conventional bank deposits and current accounts are classified as Qard for this reason.","Time value of money is recognized in genuine trade (credit price may exceed cash price) but never in Qard or Dayn repayment.","Debtors must repay promptly (wilful default is injustice); creditors are urged toward leniency but cannot be compelled to extend time or waive debt.","Husnal Qadha (voluntary excess repayment) is commendable but must never be systematized; remission for early payment is allowed only for already-due debts (Duyun Halah), not not-yet-due debts (Duyun Mu'ajjalah).","Penalty clauses for wilful default are now permitted (proceeds to charity) for Dayn but never for Qard; actual-loss compensation (not opportunity cost) may also be claimed via court/arbitration.","Hawalah (assignment of debt) is permissible because it preserves recourse to the original debtor; a pure sale of debt lacks recourse and is prohibited for Gharar/Riba.","Kafalah (suretyship) and Rihn (pledge) are the two guarantee mechanisms; the pledger retains the pledged property's risk and reward, and 'pledge cannot be foreclosed' without court process.","Bai' al-Dayn (selling debt at other than par value) is unanimously prohibited, and indexing loans/debts to inflation, gold, or currency baskets is likewise rejected as a form of Riba."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you define Qard, Salaf, Dayn and ‘Ariyah?","Can you explain why a quality benefit on a loan is Riba?","Can you explain the treatment of current accounts?","Can you distinguish Duyun Halah and Mu’ajjalah for rebates?","Can you explain penalty vs compensation on default?","Can you explain Hawalah and how it differs from sale of debt?","Can you explain risk, reward and benefit in a pledge?","Can you state the rulings on indexation?"],"topics":[{"id":"t7.1","section":"7.1","title":"Introduction: Why Debt Rules Matter for Islamic Banks","pages":[155,155],"tier":"revision","concepts":["qard","dayn"],"intuition":"If Islamic banks don’t lend at interest, why study loans? Because most of their assets are debts created by sale and lease.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banks mobilise deposits mostly on PLS (current accounts are loans or Amanah) and use PLS and debt-creating modes on the asset side — much of it debt. In Shari’ah lending is a virtuous act: only the principal is repayable.","academicExplanation":["Conventional banks take deposits and lend at interest. Islamic banks’ superstructure is PLS or modes that culminate in interest-free debt; current deposits are loans or Wadi‘ah/Amanah. Practically a large part of their assets generate debt. Loaning is a virtuous act without compensation for use of money; any excess demanded makes it usurious. Topics: objects of loans, repayment, gifts, security and surety, Hawalah, default, creditor/debtor duties, sale of debt, prepayment rebate and insolvency."],"examExplanation":"Islamic banks create debt through sale/lease; lending is virtuous — principal only.","keyPoints":["Current deposits are treated as loans (or Amanah).","Most Islamic bank assets are debt-creating (sale and lease), so Dayn rules govern them.","Current deposits are generally treated as loans (Qard), sometimes as Wadi‘ah/Amanah."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Deposits as loans","kind":"practice","text":"A customer keeps 50,000 in a current account at an Islamic bank. The bank treats it as a loan (Qard) and guarantees it, but pays no return, since any excess on a loan would be Riba (p. 155)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t7.2","t8.5.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How are current deposits generally treated in Islamic banks?","options":["As Mudarabah investments","As loans (or Wadi‘ah/Amanah)","As Salam","As Ijarah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 155."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t7.2","section":"7.2","title":"The Terms Defined: Qard, Salaf, Dayn and ‘Ariyah","pages":[155,157],"tier":"core","concepts":["qard","dayn","salaf","ariyah"],"intuition":"Loan, debt, fixed-term loan, borrowed item — Islamic law has a precise word for each.","simpleExplanation":"Qard: giving something of value to another as a virtue, to be returned in similar amount on demand. Salaf: a loan for a fixed time (also another name for Salam). Dayn: a liability from a credit transaction (sale or rent). ‘Ariyah: lending an item for use free, returning the same item.","academicExplanation":["Qard (literally “to cut” — property cut off from the lender) is giving anything of value in ownership to another by way of virtue, to be repaid in the same or similar amount on demand or at the settled time; jurists agree on this definition. Tantawi (quoted by the SAB): Qard is more particular than Dayn — a loan given as help; Dayn arises from rent, sale or purchase; debts should be returned without profit.","Salaf literally means a loan that draws no profit; more widely, loans for specified periods; it is also another name for Salam. Qard is a kind of Salaf payable on demand (Hanafi, Shafi‘i, Hanbali; Malik: a settled Qard cannot be recalled early). Dayn arises from any credit transaction deferring a counter value. ‘Ariyah: giving a commodity for use without return; the same item must be returned (the Prophet borrowed camels and breast-plates from Safwan for Hunain; Safwan waived missing plates). Qard returns a similar item.","Murabaha creates Duyun that must be returned without profit over the debt. Qard is proved by Sunnah and consensus; the Prophet borrowed 40,000 dirhams for the State; lending is more pleasing to Allah than alms and equivalent to half Sadaqah. Ownership of loaned goods passes to the borrower who may use, sell or donate them. A Qard should not be conditional on another contract like Bai‘."],"examExplanation":"Qard: virtuous loan, similar returned on demand. Salaf: fixed-term loan (also Salam). Dayn: liability from credit sale/rent. ‘Ariyah: free use, same item returned. Murabaha creates Dayn → no excess.","keyPoints":["Qard vs ‘Ariyah: similar vs same item returned.","Qard must not be conditional on a sale.","Salaf: loan for a fixed time; Qard: payable on demand (Hanafi, Shafi‘i, Hanbali view). Malik: once a time is set, earlier demand is not allowed.","A Hadith rates Qard as more pleasing to Allah than alms, or equal to half Sadaqah though it is repaid."],"definitions":[{"term":"Qard","definition":"Giving anything of value to another by way of virtue, to be repaid in same or similar amount on demand or at the settled time."},{"term":"Salaf","definition":"A loan for a fixed time that draws no profit; also another name for Salam."},{"term":"Dayn","definition":"A liability arising from a credit transaction (sale, rent) in which one counter value is deferred."},{"term":"‘Ariyah","definition":"Giving a nonconsumable commodity for use free of charge; exactly the same item must be returned."}],"examples":[{"title":"Camels and breastplates from Safwan","kind":"textbook","text":"Before Hunain the Prophet (pbuh) borrowed camels and iron breastplates from Safwan as ‘Ariyah, promising full return. When some plates were missing he offered compensation, which Safwan waived (p. 156)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1","t5.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What distinguishes ‘Ariyah from Qard?","options":["‘Ariyah carries interest","In ‘Ariyah exactly the borrowed item is returned; in Qard a similar item","Qard is for animals only","No difference"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 156."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.2","sectionTitle":"The Terms Defined","title":"Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah: Defining the Key Terms","covers":["t7.2"],"overview":"Islamic law distinguishes Qard (loan payable on demand), Salaf (loan for a fixed term), Dayn (debt arising from any credit transaction), and 'Ariyah (gratuitous loan of a nonconsumable item returned in kind).","simpleExplanation":"There are a few different Arabic words for 'owing something,' and they mean slightly different things. Qard is a loan you can be asked to pay back any time. Salaf is a loan for a fixed period. Dayn is any debt that comes from a deal -- like buying something on credit. 'Ariyah is different again: it's borrowing an actual object (like a car) that you have to give back exactly as it was, not just pay back the value.","academicExplanation":"Qard literally means 'to cut,' because the property is cut off from the lender's ownership when given to the borrower. Legally, Qard means giving something of value into another's ownership by way of virtue, so the recipient may benefit from it, on condition that the same or a similar amount is paid back on demand or at a settled time -- jurists are unanimous on this definition. Syed Mohammad Tantawi (cited via Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench) distinguishes Qard (a loan given as help/charity/advance for a certain time) from the broader Dayn (a debt incurred by rent, sale, purchase, or any other transaction) -- both must be returned without profit. Salaf literally means a loan drawing forth no profit for the creditor; in a wider sense it covers loans of specified (short/intermediate/long) terms; Salaf is also another name for Salam (advance-paid, future-delivered sale). Qard is, in fact, a particular kind of Salaf -- specifically, one payable on demand rather than at a fixed future time. Per the Hanafi, Shafi'e, and Hanbali schools, Qard is among Duyun Halah (demandable any time) -- Imam Abu Hanifa and Ibn Hazm hold any Qard can be recalled at any time; Imam Malik holds that once a repayment time is settled (Qard-e-Muajjalah), the lender cannot demand earlier payment. 'Ariyah is a separate concept: giving a commodity to another for use without taking any return -- also a virtuous act like Qard, but the borrowed commodity itself (not merely its value) is a liability of the borrower, who must return the exact item. The key structural difference: in 'Ariyah, the exact borrowed commodity must be returned; in Qard, a similar (not necessarily the same) amount/item is repaid, since ownership of the loaned item transfers fully to the borrower, who may use, sell, or donate it as he wishes. The legality of Qard is proven by Sunnah and jurist consensus; the Prophet himself borrowed 40,000 dirhams from Abdullah Ibn Rabi'ah for the Islamic State during Ghazwa Hunain, and Hadith describe Qard as more pleasing to Allah than alms-giving, and as equivalent to half Sadaqah. A Qard must not be conditioned on any other contract (like Bai').","examExplanation":"Qard = loan payable on demand (ownership of the loaned item transfers fully; similar amount repaid). Salaf = loan for a fixed term (also = another name for Salam). Dayn = a debt/liability arising from ANY credit transaction (sale, lease rentals, etc.), broader than Qard. 'Ariyah = gratuitous loan of a nonconsumable item; the EXACT same item (not a similar substitute) must be returned. Schools differ on recallability: Hanafi/Ibn Hazm -- any Qard recallable anytime; Maliki -- once a repayment date is set (Qard-e-Muajjalah), lender can't demand earlier payment. A Qard must not be conditioned on another contract (e.g. a sale).","keyPoints":["Qard: a loan given by way of virtue, repayable (in a similar amount) on demand or at a settled time; ownership of the loaned item fully transfers to the borrower.","Salaf: literally a loan drawing forth no profit; broadly covers fixed-term loans (short/intermediate/long); also another name for Salam. Qard is a particular kind of Salaf -- specifically payable on demand.","Dayn: a debt/liability arising from any credit transaction (sale, purchase, rent, etc.) -- broader in scope than Qard.","'Ariyah: gratuitous loan of a nonconsumable commodity for use, without taking any return -- the EXACT same item (not a substitute) must be returned, unlike Qard's repayment of a similar amount.","Hanafi/Shafi'e/Hanbali view: Qard is among Duyun Halah (demandable at any time) -- e.g. Imam Abu Hanifa and Ibn Hazm hold any Qard can be recalled anytime.","Maliki view: once a repayment time is settled (Qard-e-Muajjalah), the lender cannot demand earlier payment.","A Qard must not be made conditional upon any other contract, such as a sale (Bai')."],"definitions":[{"term":"Qard","definition":"To give anything of value into another's ownership by way of virtue, so the recipient may benefit from it, on condition that the same or a similar amount is paid back on demand or at a settled time."},{"term":"Salaf","definition":"Literally, a loan which draws forth no profit for the creditor; in a wider sense, covers loans for specified (short/intermediate/long) periods; also another name for Salam."},{"term":"Dayn","definition":"A debt/liability incurred by way of rent, sale, purchase, or any other transaction, to be paid back without profit; broader than Qard."},{"term":"'Ariyah","definition":"A gratuitous loan of a nonconsumable commodity for use, without taking any return; the exact borrowed item (not merely its value) is a liability of the borrower and must be returned."}],"conditions":["A Qard must not be made conditional upon any other contract, such as Bai' (sale)."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's 'Ariyah of camels and armor","kind":"textbook","text":"Preparing for Ghazwa-e-Hunain, the Prophet took camels and iron breast-plates as 'Ariyah from Safwan bin Umayyah, assuring full return; when some plates were found missing on return, Safwan (having since converted to Islam) waived the loss."}],"commonConfusions":["Qard and Dayn are often used loosely as synonyms; the chapter clarifies Dayn is the broader term (any credit-transaction-created debt), while Qard specifically denotes a loan given as help/charity, demandable on the terms above.","'Ariyah (return the exact same item) is often confused with Qard (return a similar amount/item, since ownership transfers) -- this distinction matters because 'Ariyah involves usufruct-type borrowing of a specific object, not a fungible loan."],"distinctions":["Qard (demand loan, similar item/amount repaid, ownership transfers) vs 'Ariyah (exact same item must be returned)","Qard (specific: demand-payable) vs Salaf (broader: fixed-term loans, and also = Salam) vs Dayn (broadest: any credit-transaction debt)"],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Bai' Mu'ajjal","Qard al-Hasan"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,156,157]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Lender Hands Over Ownership","description":"The lender (Muqrid) gives a sum of money or a fungible commodity to the borrower (Muqtarid) 'by way of virtue' -- this is not a sale or a rental of the item's use, but a transfer of full ownership: the property is 'cut off' from the lender's ownership (the literal meaning of Qard) and passes entirely to the borrower."},{"step":"Borrower Uses the Item Freely","description":"Because ownership -- not just possession -- has transferred, the borrower may use, sell, or even donate the borrowed sum/commodity as he wishes. This is the key structural difference from 'Ariyah, where a commodity is lent for use only: there, the exact item itself remains a liability the borrower must return, and he cannot dispose of it as his own."},{"step":"Borrower Owes Only an Identical Replacement","description":"The borrower's obligation is to pay back 'the same or similar amount of that thing' -- no more, no less. He does not need to return the identical unit he received (unlike 'Ariyah), only a like-for-like replacement of the same quantity and kind, and the amount/value of the loan must be known without doubt."},{"step":"Repayment on Demand or at the Settled Time","description":"If the loan is a straightforward Qard, the lender may demand repayment at any time (per the Hanafi, Shafi'e and Hanbali view that Qard is among Duyun Halah); per the Maliki view, once a repayment time is settled (Qard-e-Muajjalah), the lender cannot demand earlier payment. If instead the loan is for a fixed term, it is termed Salaf rather than Qard."},{"step":"Any Voluntary Excess Must Not Be a Precondition","description":"The borrower may voluntarily repay more than the principal, or give a gift, as Husnal Qadha (gracious repayment) -- not by way of 'Ariyah or Hibah, but to recover it in the form in which it was given, per the Maliki school's characterization of Qard as a favour granted to the borrower alone. Such voluntary generosity, not stipulated as a condition of the loan, is not Riba; but a loan conditioned on receiving anything in excess, explicit or implicit, is unlawful Riba, and a Qard must not be made conditional upon any other contract, such as Bai' (sale)."}]}],"table":{"caption":"Loan-related terms (Section 7.2)","head":["Term","Arises from","Repayment"],"rows":[["Qard","Benevolent loan","Same/similar, on demand"],["Salaf","Fixed-term loan (also Salam)","At settled time"],["Dayn","Credit sale, due rent, other credit transactions","At stipulated time, no excess"],["‘Ariyah","Free loan of an item for use","The same item returned"]]}},{"id":"t7.3","section":"7.3","title":"Illegality of Commercial Interest","pages":[157,158],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba"],"intuition":"Is any benefit to a lender acceptable if it isn’t cash — a better coin, a favour, a lease? No.","simpleExplanation":"Contemporary scholars agree that commercial interest is Riba. Any loan stipulating a benefit over the principal — in quantity, quality, a gift, or a condition that the borrower lease his house or sell something to the lender — is Riba.","academicExplanation":["Contemporary scholars have reached consensus that modern commercial interest is Riba; no interest-based loan is exempt. Jahiliyyah Riba took various forms — increase on loans or on debts from sales, charged periodically or with principal — all called Riba. All loans stipulating benefit over principal are void, whether in quantity or quality (Sarakhsi; Ibn Qudama: all jurists agree). Ibn al-Munzar: unanimous that any binding increase, gift or present above principal is Riba. Ibn Abbas and Ibn Masud forbade loans entailing benefit because lending is mutual kindness. Lending debased coins for un-debased, or conditioning the loan on renting a house, selling something or a future loan, is illegal (sale and loan combined)."],"examExplanation":"Commercial interest = Riba (consensus). Any stipulated benefit — quantity, quality, gift, or ancillary contract — makes a loan usurious.","keyPoints":["Benefit in quality also counts (debased for good coins).","Conditioning a loan on another contract is prohibited.","Ibn Abbas and Ibn Masud forbade loans carrying benefit because a loan is a contract of kindness."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Hidden benefits in a loan","kind":"textbook","text":"A loan of 1,000 on condition that the borrower rents his house to the lender, sells him something, or repays in better-quality coins is Riba, even though the principal is unchanged. Ibn Abbas and Ibn Masud forbade loans that carry any benefit (pp. 157–158)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A loan benefit is Riba only if it is extra money.","right":"Any stipulated benefit — more quantity, better quality (good coins for debased ones), a lease, sale or another loan — makes the loan usurious (Ibn Qudama, Ibn al-Munzar) (pp. 157–158)."}],"relatedTopics":["t3.2.1","t7.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A lender makes a loan conditional on the borrower renting his house to the lender. This is:","options":["Permissible","Illegal — a stipulated benefit/combined contract","Hawalah","Kafalah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 158."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.3","sectionTitle":"Illegality of Commercial Interest; Loaning and the Banking System","title":"Illegality of Commercial Interest and Its Implications for Banking","covers":["t7.3"],"overview":"Contemporary scholarly consensus holds modern commercial interest falls squarely under Riba; any loan condition creating benefit above the principal -- in quantity or quality -- is illegal, which has direct implications for how conventional deposit/lending structures are classified.","simpleExplanation":"Scholars today agree that modern bank interest is exactly the kind of Riba the Qur'an forbids -- no version of it gets a pass. Any extra benefit built into a loan as a condition, even something small like slightly better coins instead of the same ones, counts as forbidden. Because conventional bank deposits promise a guaranteed or fixed return with the principal protected, they count as this kind of loan (Qard) under Islamic law -- so charging or paying interest on them is not allowed.","academicExplanation":"Contemporary Shari'ah scholars have reached consensus that modern commercial interest falls under Riba, with no loan/debt based on interest exempt. Historically, Jahiliyyah-era Riba took various forms -- sometimes arising from a sale-created debt, sometimes from a loan; sometimes charged periodically with principal due at maturity, sometimes charged together with principal -- all termed Riba. The governing rule: any loan embodying a benefit over the principal as a precondition is void, regardless of whether the increase is in quantity or quality. Al-Sarakhsi: a loan carrying a benefit stipulated as a precondition is a prohibited loan. Ibn Qudama (Al-Mughni): all jurists agree any loan conditioned to give an increase is illegal, whether the increase is in quantity or quality -- e.g. lending debased coins on condition of repayment in sound coins, or lending currency for repayment in 'better units,' is illegal. Ibn Abbas and Ibn Masud forbade loans entailing benefit because a loan is fundamentally a contract of mutual kindness; a clause effecting any increase 'kills the spirit of lending.' Tying a loan to an unrelated benefit (e.g. requiring the borrower to rent his house to the lender, or sell him something, or extend a future loan) is likewise illegal, since the Prophet prohibited making a sale contract conditional on a loan. On the banking system (7.4): conventional bank deposits and government securities fall under Qard's definition because principal is guaranteed and a fixed or activity-independent return is stipulated; banks use these funds as they wish and remain fully liable for repayment even on loss. Current accounts are similarly categorized as loans, since banks are equally liable to current-account holders as to fixed-account holders. On the assets side, conventional financing (consumption loans, working capital, trade/project finance, BMR, SME finance, government finance) mostly takes the form of interest-based loans/debts. Most Islamic finance writers hold Islamic banks will continue as intermediaries, though some recommend they also act as traders/institutions dealing in tangible goods (universal banking/holding-company models with subsidiaries). Regardless of structure, Islamic banks must not earn money from money -- they must be involved with real goods, using trade- and lease-based modes that create debt governed by Dayn rules.","examExplanation":"Consensus: modern commercial interest = Riba, no exceptions. Rule: ANY loan condition creating a benefit above principal -- quantity or quality -- is void (Al-Sarakhsi, Ibn Qudama/Al-Mughni citing unanimous jurist agreement). Conventional deposits/government securities = classified as Qard (principal guaranteed + fixed/activity-independent return); banks bear full repayment liability even on loss. Current accounts = also Qard-type loans. Islamic banks must avoid earning money from money -- must use trade/lease-based, debt-creating (Dayn) modes tied to real goods, even while potentially remaining intermediaries.","keyPoints":["Contemporary consensus: modern commercial interest is Riba without exception, regardless of the loan/debt's form.","Any loan condition creating a benefit above the principal -- whether in quantity or quality -- is void (unanimous jurist agreement per Ibn Qudama's Al-Mughni).","Tying an unrelated benefit to a loan (e.g. requiring the borrower to rent his house to the lender, sell him something, or extend a future loan) is illegal -- the Prophet prohibited combining a sale contract with a loan condition.","Conventional bank deposits and government securities are classified as Qard because principal is guaranteed and a fixed/activity-independent return is stipulated.","Current accounts are also categorized as loans (Qard) -- banks are equally liable to current-account holders as to fixed-account holders.","Islamic banks may continue functioning as intermediaries (some scholars recommend they also act as traders in tangible goods via universal-banking/holding-company structures), but must not earn money from money.","Islamic banks' asset-side financing largely creates debt through trade- and lease-based modes, governed by the rules of Dayn."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A loan must not carry any stipulated benefit above the principal, whether that benefit is in quantity or quality.","A loan must not be conditioned on the borrower providing an unrelated benefit (e.g. a rental, a sale, or a future loan) to the lender."],"principles":["Any binding condition on the borrower entailing an increase, gift, or present above the principal is Riba (per Ibn al-Munzar, cited by Ibn Qudama)."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Debased-coin loan condition","kind":"textbook","text":"Lending debased coins on condition of repayment in sound/un-debased coins, or lending currency units for repayment in 'better units' in future, is illegal -- the increase in quality itself constitutes Riba."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think only a monetary percentage increase counts as Riba; the chapter is explicit that ANY stipulated benefit -- even a qualitative one, like better coins or a bundled favor -- makes a loan usurious."],"distinctions":["Loan-conditioned benefit (illegal, regardless of quantity/quality) vs a genuinely voluntary, unconditioned gracious repayment (permitted -- covered in Husnal Qadha)"],"relatedConcepts":["Riba Al-Nasiah","Qard al-Hasan","Current accounts","Dayn"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.3","pages":[157,158,159]}}]},{"id":"t7.4","section":"7.4","title":"Loaning and the Banking System","pages":[158,158],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["qard","islamic-banking"],"intuition":"Where do deposits and bonds fit in this framework?","simpleExplanation":"Conventional deposits and government securities are Qard: principal guaranteed plus a return not linked to outcomes. Conventional banks’ financing is mostly loans. Islamic banks remain intermediaries but deal in real goods; their trade and lease modes create debt governed by the rules of Dayn.","academicExplanation":["Deposits with conventional banks and investments in government securities are Qard: principal guaranteed and a return fixed or unrelated to outcomes; banks are liable even in loss. Current accounts are also loans. Conventional asset-side financing — working capital, trade, project, BMR, SME, government — is mostly loans or debts. Most writers hold banks will remain intermediaries; some recommend they also act as traders, adopting universal banking or holding-company models with subsidiaries/mutual funds. Whatever the structure, Islamic banks should not earn money from money and must abide by the rules of Dayn."],"examExplanation":"Conventional deposits/govt securities = Qard with return → Riba. Islamic banks: intermediaries dealing in real goods; debts subject to Dayn rules.","keyPoints":["Universal banking/holding company models suggested.","Conventional deposits and government securities are Qard: principal guaranteed plus a return not linked to outcome.","Islamic banks may remain intermediaries, but must deal in real goods and not earn money from money; some propose universal banking or holding-company models."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Same deposit, two banks","kind":"practice","text":"A saver places 100,000 for one year. With a conventional bank it is a loan: principal and interest are owed whatever the bank earns. With an Islamic bank on Mudarabah, the return depends on the bank’s actual results (p. 158)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"A current account is a trust deposit, not a loan.","right":"Banks are as liable to current account holders as to fixed account holders, so conventional current accounts are loans (p. 158)."}],"relatedTopics":["t8.4.1","t4.2.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why are conventional deposits categorised as Qard?","options":["They are gifts","Principal is guaranteed and the return is not linked to business outcomes","They are leases","They are partnerships"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 158."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t7.5","section":"7.5","title":"Guidance from the Holy Qur’an on Loans and Debts","pages":[159,159],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["documentation"],"intuition":"Why do Islamic banks document credit so carefully? The longest verse of the Qur’an tells them to.","simpleExplanation":"Verse 2:282–283 instructs that credit transactions for a fixed period be written down by a scribe, dictated by the debtor, with two witnesses; on a journey without a scribe, a pledge with possession may serve. Spot transactions need not be written.","academicExplanation":["The verse: when dealing in lending or future obligations for a fixed period, reduce it to writing; the liable party dictates; a guardian dictates for the weak; two male witnesses or one man and two women; do not disdain writing, small or big; spot transactions need not be written but take witnesses in commercial contracts; neither scribe nor witness should be harmed; on a journey without a scribe, a pledge with possession. Credit transactions require documentation and witnesses (Ibn Hazm: witnesses necessary). The verse covers Salam and Bai‘ Mu’ajjal and teaches business as if in the presence of God."],"examExplanation":"2:282–283: write down fixed-period credit, debtor dictates, witnesses; pledge with possession when no scribe; spot deals need not be written.","keyPoints":["Covers Salam and Bai‘ Mu’ajjal.","Ibn Hazm considers witnesses necessary in credit transactions.","Salam and Bai‘ Mu’ajjal are covered by this verse."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"The longest verse (2:282–283)","kind":"textbook","text":"Credit transactions should be written down by a scribe with the liable party dictating and two witnesses; spot transactions need no writing; on a journey without a scribe, a pledge with possession may serve (p. 159)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.2.8","t7.15"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to 2:283, what may serve if one is on a journey and cannot find a scribe?","options":["An oral promise","A pledge with possession","Interest","Nothing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 159."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.5","sectionTitle":"Qur'anic Guidance on Loans and Debts; The Substance of Loans","title":"Qur'anic Guidance on Documentation, and What Can Validly Be Loaned","covers":["t7.5"],"overview":"The Qur'an (2:282-283) instructs documenting and witnessing credit transactions; the substance of a valid Qard is anything Mubah (permissible), valuable, and available in kind for repayment, with schools differing on whether nonfungibles may be loaned.","simpleExplanation":"The Qur'an tells people to write down and get witnesses for deals involving future payment (like loans), while everyday spot transactions don't need this. As for what can actually be lent under Qard, it has to be something useful and permissible that has a like-for-like substitute available -- so you can pay back an equivalent. Scholars differ on whether you can lend things that aren't 'identical units,' like a specific animal, but the Prophet did lend/borrow animals, so most schools allow it.","academicExplanation":"Qur'an 2:282-283 instructs believers, when dealing in lending or future-obligation transactions, to reduce the terms to writing via a faithful scribe, have the liable party dictate the terms without diminishing what he owes (or his guardian dictate if he is deficient/weak), and secure two male witnesses (or one man and two women). The verse distinguishes documented credit transactions (future payment) from spot transactions (no writing required, though oral witnesses/trust between parties suffice); if traveling without a scribe, a pledge with possession (Rihn Maqbudah) may serve the purpose. This covers Salam (advance payment, future delivery) and Bai' Mu'ajjal (future payment) transactions, and reflects the Qur'an's guidance toward the highest standard of commercial morality, as though every transaction were conducted in the presence of God. On the substance of loans (7.6): besides Thaman-type goods, Qard can be given in anything Mubah (permissible), of value, with a similar available for repayment. The Hanafi school restricts loans to fungibles (similars by weight/measure), though the Sunnah shows the Prophet accepted Qard of animals (nonfungibles); the other three schools (Maliki, Shafi'e, Hanbali) allow Qard of any saleable commodity except human beings, provided the amount/value of the loan is known without doubt. The Maliki school specifically defines Qard as a loan of something 'valuable,' granted only as a favour (distinguishing it from 'Ariyah or Hibah), to be recovered in the same form granted -- the term 'valuable' excludes worthless items, and 'favour' clarifies the benefit accrues to the borrower alone, not the lender. Under this view, Qard may consist of merchandise, animals, or similars of weight -- essentially anything that could be the subject of Salam.","examExplanation":"Qur'an 2:282-283: document + witness (2 men, or 1 man + 2 women) future-obligation/credit transactions; spot transactions need no writing. Traveling without a scribe: a pledge with possession (Rihn Maqbudah) suffices. Applies to Salam and Bai' Mu'ajjal. Substance of loans: Hanafi -- Qard restricted to fungibles (similars by weight/measure) only; other 3 schools (Maliki/Shafi'e/Hanbali) -- Qard allowed for any saleable item except humans, provided amount/value is known without doubt. Maliki definition of Qard emphasizes 'valuable' (excludes worthless items) and 'favour' (benefit is for the borrower alone).","keyPoints":["Qur'an 2:282-283 instructs documenting (via a faithful scribe) and witnessing (2 men, or 1 man + 2 women) future-obligation/credit transactions; spot transactions require no writing.","If traveling without access to a scribe, a pledge with possession (Rihn Maqbudah) may serve the purpose instead.","This guidance covers Salam and Bai' Mu'ajjal transactions, reflecting the Qur'an's standard of highest commercial morality.","Hanafi school: Qard restricted to fungibles only (similars by weight/measure); other schools (Maliki, Shafi'e, Hanbali) allow Qard of any saleable item except human beings.","The loaned item's amount and value must be known without any doubt, regardless of school.","Maliki definition of Qard: a loan of something 'valuable,' granted only as a favour (distinct from 'Ariyah/Hibah) -- benefit accrues to the borrower alone, recoverable in the same form granted."],"definitions":[{"term":"Rihn Maqbudah","definition":"A pledge held with possession -- the Qur'an-sanctioned alternative to written documentation when a scribe is unavailable, e.g. while traveling."}],"conditions":["The amount and value of a loaned item must be known without any doubt.","Documentation (via a faithful scribe) and witnessing are required for credit/future-obligation transactions; spot transactions are exempt."],"principles":[],"processSteps":["For a credit/future-payment transaction: engage a faithful scribe to write the terms.","The liable party (or his guardian, if deficient/weak) dictates the terms, without diminishing what he owes.","Secure two witnesses (two men, or one man and two women).","If no scribe is available (e.g. while traveling), a pledge with possession (Rihn Maqbudah) may serve instead."],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think the Qur'an requires written documentation for ALL transactions; the verse explicitly exempts on-the-spot transactions from the writing requirement, though it still recommends witnesses even for these.","The Hanafi restriction of Qard to fungibles only is sometimes overgeneralized as the universal Islamic rule; the other three major schools permit lending nonfungibles too (e.g. animals), consistent with the Prophet's own practice."],"distinctions":["Hanafi view (Qard limited to fungibles) vs Maliki/Shafi'e/Hanbali view (Qard permitted for any saleable item except humans)"],"relatedConcepts":["Salam","Bai' Mu'ajjal","Mithli/Qimi goods","Rihn"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.5","pages":[159,160]}}]},{"id":"t7.6","section":"7.6","title":"The Substance of Loans","pages":[159,160],"tier":"detailed","concepts":["qard"],"intuition":"What can be lent?","simpleExplanation":"Qard can be given in anything permissible, valuable and whose similar is available for repayment. Hanafis restrict it to fungibles; other schools allow any saleable commodity (the Sunnah shows loans of animals). The amount and value must be known.","academicExplanation":["Besides Thaman, Qard can be given in Mubah goods of value whose similar is available. Hanafis: fungibles (weight and measure) only; but the Sunnah shows loans of animals. Other schools: any commodity that can be sold, except human beings. The amount and value must be known. Maliki definition: a loan of something valuable granted as a favour (not ‘Ariyah or Hibah) to recover in the same form — benefit for the borrower alone; anything that can be the subject of Salam may be lent."],"examExplanation":"Qard in permissible, valuable goods with available similars; Hanafi: fungibles only; others: any saleable item; amount known.","keyPoints":["Maliki: benefit is for the borrower alone.","Malikis: Qard is a loan of something valuable, only as a favour to the borrower — anything that can be the subject of Salam."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Lending an animal","kind":"textbook","text":"The Sunnah records loans of animals. The Hanafis limit Qard to fungibles measured by weight or volume, while other schools allow any saleable commodity (pp. 159–160)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t7.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which school restricts Qard to fungibles?","options":["Maliki","Hanafi","Shafi‘i","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 159."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Hanafi view","b":"Other three schools","text":"Hanafis allow Qard only in fungibles (weight and measure); the others allow lending anything that can be sold (except humans), citing the Prophet’s borrowing of animals (pp. 159–160)."}]},{"id":"t7.7","section":"7.7","title":"Repayment of the Principal Only","pages":[160,160],"tier":"core","concepts":["qard","riba"],"intuition":"Can a lender accept a gift from the borrower?","simpleExplanation":"Debt must be repaid — even martyrs are not forgiven debt — and nothing above principal may be exacted, whoever the parties are. After lending, the creditor should not accept presents unless such exchanges were customary before. Cost-free customary benefits (e.g. repayment in another city) are allowed.","academicExplanation":["Lending is unavoidable and permissible, but should not fund lavish consumption. A loan must be paid; debt is not forgiven even for martyrs. Any excess exacted is Riba, with no exemption for Muslim/non-Muslim, employer/employee or State/people. The creditor should refrain from accepting a present unless customary before the loan. Customary indirect benefits costing the borrower nothing are permissible — e.g. agreeing repayment in another country where in both parties’ interest (Ibn Zubair accepted money in Makkah to be paid in Iraq via his brother; Ibn Abbas and Ali did not object)."],"examExplanation":"Principal only; no presents unless customary pre-loan; cost-free customary benefits (e.g. repayment elsewhere) allowed.","keyPoints":["No exemption based on the parties’ identities.","Suftajah-like remittance arrangements allowed."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Paying a debt in another city","kind":"textbook","text":"Ibn Zubair took money in Makkah to be paid in Iraq by drafts on his brother Mus‘ab; Ibn Abbas and Ali did not object. Customary indirect benefits costing the borrower nothing are allowed (p. 160)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Interest is allowed between a State and its citizens or between a Muslim and a non-Muslim.","right":"There is no exemption for any relationship — Muslim/non-Muslim, employer/employee or State/people (p. 160)."}],"relatedTopics":["t7.11","t3.2.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A borrower offers the lender a present that they never exchanged before the loan. The lender should:","options":["Accept it","Refrain from accepting it","Charge interest instead","Demand more"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 160."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.7","sectionTitle":"Repayment of the Principal Only; Time Value of Money in Loans and Debts","title":"Repayment of Principal Only, and Why Time Value Doesn't Apply to Loans","covers":["t7.7"],"overview":"A loan must be repaid at exactly its principal amount, with no exemptions; unlike in trade, no value can be assigned to the time given for payment of a Qard or Dayn once the amount is mutually fixed.","simpleExplanation":"Borrowing is a normal part of life and is allowed, but debt is serious -- it must always be repaid, with no shortcuts, and demanding anything above the exact amount borrowed is forbidden, no matter who's involved (Muslim or non-Muslim, employer or employee, government or citizen). Unlike in ordinary trading, where a credit price can legitimately be higher than a cash price, once a loan amount is fixed, you can't add anything extra just because time passed before repayment -- money is only exchanged equally for the same money.","academicExplanation":"Lending/borrowing is unavoidable in human life and therefore permissible -- the Prophet himself borrowed both for the Islamic State and privately -- though Islam discourages borrowing for lavish consumption. A loan must be paid; debt is not forgiven even for martyrs. A loan whereby anything in excess of the principal is exacted becomes unlawful (Riba), with no exemption based on the parties' identities (Muslim/non-Muslim, employer/employee, State/people). Prohibition of Riba means money can be lent with no expectation of return above the principal; every loan drawing forth or stipulating profit is unlawful. The Prophet said a creditor must refrain from even accepting a gift from a borrower after the loan, unless such gift exchange was already customary between them before the loan; however, indirect, costless customary benefits (e.g. repaying the debt in a different country if convenient for both parties, as Ibn Zubair did via drafts on his brother in Iraq, unopposed by Ibn Abbas and Ali) are permissible. On time value of money (7.8): while a credit price may legitimately differ from a cash price in trading, no value can be assigned to the time given for payment of a receivable once its amount is mutually stipulated in a Qard or Dayn context -- because money is not a lawful commodity for homogeneous exchange except in equal sums (e.g. $100 must be exchanged for exactly $100). Time valuation is approved by the Shari'ah in business/trade (as detailed in Chapter 4) but not in Qard or Dayn, which are virtuous acts from which no benefit may be taken. What is prohibited specifically is claiming time value of money as a predetermined quantity at a predetermined rate, unrelated to real-sector business. Currency notes are all factually homogeneous (representing purchasing power, being legal tender of the same genus/'Illah, just as dinars and dirhams were); therefore a person avoiding Riba cannot sell or lease currency notes as if they were ordinary goods/assets. Government securities, bonds, and savings certificates similarly command value like gold/silver and are thus 'money'; while gold/silver may serve as both Thaman and Mabi', bonds/currency notes reflect value only and cannot serve as Mabi' (they have no value absent government authority).","examExplanation":"A loan (Qard/Dayn) must be repaid at exactly the principal amount -- no exemptions based on the parties involved. A creditor may not even accept a gift from a borrower post-loan unless such gifting predates the loan and was already customary (indirect, costless customary benefits like cross-border repayment convenience ARE allowed). Time value of money: recognized in TRADE (credit price ≠ cash price is fine), but NOT in Qard/Dayn -- once a loan/debt amount is fixed, no additional value can attach to the delay in payment; $100 must be repaid as exactly $100. Currency notes cannot be sold/leased like ordinary goods since they're homogeneous, purchasing-power-representing legal tender.","keyPoints":["Borrowing/lending is permissible (even Prophetically modeled) but discouraged for lavish consumption; a loan must always be repaid -- debt is not forgiven even for martyrs.","Any excess above the principal exacted from a loan is Riba, with no exemption based on the identity of the parties (Muslim/non-Muslim, employer/employee, State/citizen).","A creditor must refrain from even accepting a gift from a borrower after the loan, unless such gift-exchange predates and was already customary between them.","Indirect, costless customary benefits (e.g. repaying a debt conveniently in a different country/via drafts) are permissible.","Time value of money is recognized in trade (credit price may exceed cash price) but NOT in Qard or Dayn, which are virtuous acts yielding no benefit to the lender.","Once a Qard/Dayn amount is mutually stipulated, no additional value attaches to the payment delay -- $100 must be repaid as exactly $100.","Currency notes are homogeneous (same genus/'Illah, representing purchasing power) and cannot be sold or leased like ordinary goods.","Government securities/bonds/savings certificates command value like gold/silver (hence are 'money'), but unlike gold/silver (which can be both Thaman and Mabi'), they reflect value only and cannot serve as Mabi'."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A loan (Qard/Dayn) must be repaid without any excess above the principal, regardless of the identity of the parties involved.","A creditor may accept a gift from a borrower after lending only if such gift-giving was already customary between them before the loan."],"principles":["Time valuation is approved by the Shari'ah in business/trade, but not in Qard or Dayn, since the latter are virtuous acts from which no benefit can be taken."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ibn Zubair's cross-border repayment convenience","kind":"textbook","text":"Ibn Zubair accepted sums from Makkah inhabitants to be repaid in Iraq through drafts drawn on his brother Mus'ab there -- an indirect, customary, costless convenience unopposed by Ibn Abbas and Ali, illustrating a permissible indirect benefit distinct from Riba."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume that since credit prices can legitimately exceed cash prices in trade, a similar 'delay premium' should be allowed on a loan; the chapter is explicit this does NOT apply to Qard/Dayn -- time value of money is recognized only in genuine trade/business, not in loan/debt repayment.","Accepting ANY gift from a borrower post-loan seems harmless but is restricted -- only pre-existing customary gift exchange (unrelated to the loan) is permitted."],"distinctions":["Time value of money recognized in trade (credit vs cash price) vs NOT recognized in Qard/Dayn (must repay exact principal, no delay premium)"],"relatedConcepts":["Riba Al-Nasiah","Al-Sarf","Currency exchange"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.7","pages":[160,161]},"calculations":[{"title":"Practice Example — generated for learning: Qard Hasan vs. an Interest-Bearing Loan of the Same Amount","formula":"Qard Repayment = Principal only (no addition permitted as a condition); Conventional Loan Repayment = Principal + (Principal x Interest Rate x Time)","inputs":[{"label":"Amount borrowed (principal)","value":"$10,000"},{"label":"Tenor","value":"12 months"},{"label":"Qard Hasan condition","value":"Borrower repays exactly the amount borrowed, no more and no less"},{"label":"Illustrative conventional interest rate (simple, p.a.)","value":"8%"}],"steps":["Under Qard Hasan, the lender gives $10,000 into the borrower's ownership 'by way of virtue' -- since it is a loan (Qard/Dayn), no value can be assigned to the 12 months the borrower holds the money once the amount is mutually stipulated: the borrower owes back exactly $10,000, because money is exchanged only in equal sums ($100 for $100, per the chapter's own illustration).","Qard Hasan repayment after 12 months = $10,000 (principal only) + $0 = $10,000.","Under a conventional interest-bearing loan of the same $10,000 at an illustrative 8% p.a. simple rate for 12 months, interest = $10,000 x 8% x 1 = $800.","Conventional loan repayment after 12 months = $10,000 + $800 = $10,800.","Difference between the two repayment amounts = $10,800 - $10,000 = $800 -- an amount 'drawn forth' by the lender purely for the passage of time, with the principal itself unchanged."],"result":"The Qard Hasan borrower repays exactly $10,000; the conventional borrower repays $10,800 for an identical $10,000 principal over the identical 12 months -- an $800 excess tied only to time.","interpretation":"The chapter states plainly that 'a loan whereby anything in excess of the principal is exacted becomes unlawful, as it amounts to Riba' and that 'no value can be assigned to the time given for payment of a receivable once its amount is mutually stipulated' in a Qard or Dayn context, because time valuation is approved by the Shari'ah in business/trade but not in Qard or Dayn -- these being 'virtuous acts from which one cannot take any benefit.' The $800 in this example is exactly that kind of excess: a predetermined quantity, calculable at a predetermined rate, unrelated to any real-sector trade or risk-bearing, charged solely for time on a loan of money for money. That is why it is Riba and forbidden, while the identical $10,000-for-$10,000 Qard Hasan repayment is not, regardless of how long the 12 months' delay was.","generated":true,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.7-7.8","pages":[160,161]}}]}]},{"id":"t7.8","section":"7.8","title":"Time Value of Money in Loans and Debts","pages":[160,161],"tier":"core","concepts":["time-value"],"intuition":"Credit price can exceed cash price — so why can’t a debt grow with time?","simpleExplanation":"Once a receivable’s amount is stipulated, no value can be assigned to the time given for payment; $100 is exchanged only for $100. Shari’ah recognises time value in business, not as a predetermined rate unrelated to real business. Currency notes and government bonds are money, not saleable or leasable goods.","academicExplanation":["While credit and cash prices may differ in trading, no value can be assigned to time for a receivable once stipulated, because money is not a lawful commodity for homogeneous exchange except in equal sums. Time valuation is approved in trade, not in Qard or Dayn. What is prohibited is claiming time value as a predetermined quantity at a predetermined rate unrelated to real business. Currency notes are homogeneous — same genus and ‘Illah — so one cannot sell or lease them to avoid Riba. Government securities, bonds and savings certificates are money; gold and silver may be Thaman and Mabi‘, but bonds and notes only reflect value and cannot be Mabi‘."],"examExplanation":"No time value on a stipulated receivable; time value only through business/trade. Notes and bonds = money, not Mabi‘; cannot be sold or leased for gain.","keyPoints":["$100 for $100 only.","Bonds and savings certificates are money."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"$100 is $100","kind":"textbook","text":"Someone who owes $100 due in a year owes exactly $100, no more for the time allowed. By contrast, a car sold on credit may carry a higher price than for cash, because time is priced into goods, not money (pp. 160–161)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Leasing out currency notes avoids Riba.","right":"Currency notes and government bonds are money; they cannot be sold or leased like goods to earn time value (p. 161)."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Why can currency notes not be leased to earn a return?","options":["They are too valuable","They are money (Thaman), not subjects of sale or lease like goods","Leasing is prohibited","They are gold"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 161."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t7.9","section":"7.9","title":"Instructions for the Debtor","pages":[161,162],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["debtor-duties"],"intuition":"What does Shari’ah demand of a borrower?","simpleExplanation":"The debtor must repay as promised; wilful delay by one able to pay is injustice and may be punished. The Prophet did not pray the funeral of a debtor until his debt was assumed. A debtor must intend to repay and thank the creditor. Authorities should make laws and standards to reduce non-payment.","academicExplanation":["The debtor’s foremost duty is repayment (17:34). Procrastination is injustice; an able debtor who does not pay may be arrested and embarrassed; leaving unpaid debt after death is a great sin. In Qard the creditor may ask for repayment even before the promised date. A genuinely unable debtor should take the creditor into confidence. A believer’s soul remains encumbered until debt is paid; the best are best in paying liabilities; the Prophet did not offer funeral prayer until a Companion’s debt was taken over. Wilful defaulters may be arrested, punished and dealt with harshly. NPLs worldwide show borrowers do not try to repay while living lavishly; borrowers must intend to repay, pay on time and thank the creditor; authorities should make laws, accounting and auditing standards to reduce moral hazard."],"examExplanation":"Repay as promised; wilful delay = injustice, punishable; intend to repay; thank creditor; laws/standards to curb NPLs.","keyPoints":["Qard may be demanded before the promised date.","A debtor able to pay who delays may be arrested and embarrassed; the soul stays encumbered until the debt is paid.","Borrowers must intend to repay and should thank the creditor."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Funeral prayer and unpaid debt","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) did not lead a funeral prayer for a Companion until someone took over his debt — at a time when the State could not pay such debts (p. 161)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t7.10","t7.13"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How did the Prophet (pbuh) treat the funeral of a Companion who died in debt?","options":["Prayed immediately","Did not offer the funeral prayer until the debt was taken over by someone","Waived the debt","Ordered his property confiscated"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 161."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.9","sectionTitle":"Instructions for the Debtor; Instructions for the Creditor","title":"Shari'ah Instructions for Debtors and Creditors","covers":["t7.9"],"overview":"Debtors are duty-bound to repay promptly and face serious warnings for wilful default; creditors are urged toward leniency, more time, or waiver for a genuinely struggling debtor, though not compelled to grant it.","simpleExplanation":"If you borrow, you must pay back on time -- deliberately delaying when you can afford to pay is treated as a real injustice, and there are strong religious warnings about dying with unpaid debt. But if a debtor genuinely can't pay, they should be honest about it with the lender rather than hide. On the flip side, lenders are strongly encouraged to be patient, give more time, or even forgive part of a debt for someone in real difficulty -- though a lender isn't legally forced to do this.","academicExplanation":"The debtor's foremost duty is to repay the loan in fulfilment of the promise/contract made. Wilful default or procrastination in payment is equated by the Prophet to injustice; a debtor able to pay but refusing can be arrested and embarrassed, and leaving unpaid debt (with no one to settle it) after death is described as the greatest sin after the Kaba'ir. In Qard, the creditor may ask for repayment even before the promised date. A debtor genuinely unable to pay should take the creditor into confidence and express regret rather than default silently; the Prophet warned a believer's soul remains encumbered by debt until paid, and praised the one best at repaying liabilities -- he reportedly withheld funeral prayer for a Companion until someone took over his debt (reflecting a period the Islamic State itself could not cover such debts). Wilful defaulters may be arrested, punished, and dealt with harshly. Modern nonperforming-loan trends are noted as reflecting borrowers' lack of serious repayment effort despite continued lavish lifestyles; borrowers must have a firm intention to repay when taking a loan, and should express gratitude when repaying. On creditor instructions (7.10): the Qur'an encourages creditors to give more time, or even waive the amount, for a debtor in dire straits; the Prophet praised lending highly and encouraged politeness and partial waiver -- e.g. asking Ka'ab Ibn Malik to waive half the debt owed by Abu Hadrad, with the remainder to be repaid however the debtor could manage. Majority view (especially Maliki, Shafi'e): no punishment/arrest for debtors genuinely in trouble; more time should be given instead. Imam Abu Hanifa: a nonpaying debtor should be imprisoned two to three months, after which his repayment capability is assessed, and he may be acquitted if genuinely unable. For a debt with a settled due date, the creditor cannot demand earlier repayment absent debtor transgression. However, if the creditor declines to grant more time, he cannot be compelled to -- the debtor must find a way to pay one way or another; even a destitute debtor has no automatic right to more time, and anything he earns beyond normal food needs must go toward repayment.","examExplanation":"Debtor duties: repay promptly; wilful default = injustice (Prophetic warning); can be arrested/embarrassed if able but unwilling; unpaid debt after death (with no payer) = grave sin. Creditor in Qard can demand repayment even before the promised date. A genuinely struggling debtor should communicate honestly rather than default silently. Creditor instructions: Qur'an encourages leniency/waiver for genuine hardship; majority (Maliki/Shafi'e) opposes punishing genuinely struggling debtors (favor more time); Abu Hanifa allows 2-3 months imprisonment then reassessment. Creditor CANNOT be forced to extend time if unwilling; even a destitute debtor has no automatic right to more time -- earnings beyond basic food needs must go to repayment.","keyPoints":["The debtor's foremost duty is prompt repayment; wilful default/procrastination is equated to injustice by the Prophet.","A debtor able to pay but refusing can be arrested and embarrassed; leaving unpaid debt after death (with no one to pay it) is the greatest sin after the Kaba'ir.","In Qard, the creditor may demand repayment even before the originally promised date.","A debtor genuinely unable to pay should communicate this honestly to the creditor rather than default silently.","The Qur'an encourages creditors to give more time or even waive the loan for a debtor in genuine dire straits; the Prophet modeled partial waiver (e.g. the Ka'ab-Abu Hadrad case).","Majority view (Maliki, Shafi'e): genuinely struggling debtors should not be punished/arrested -- more time should be given instead; Abu Hanifa allows temporary imprisonment (2-3 months) followed by reassessment.","A creditor cannot be compelled to extend more time if he declines to; even a destitute debtor has no automatic right to more time, and earnings beyond basic food needs must go toward repayment."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["For a debt with a settled due date, the creditor cannot demand earlier repayment unless the debtor has transgressed the agreed terms."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ka'ab Ibn Malik waives half of Abu Hadrad's debt","kind":"textbook","text":"When Abu Hadrad couldn't pay his debt to Ka'ab, the Prophet asked Ka'ab to waive half; Ka'ab agreed, and the Prophet then told Abu Hadrad to arrange payment of the remaining half from wherever he could."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think Islamic law always favors the debtor; while creditors are strongly encouraged toward leniency, they cannot be compelled to grant more time, and a destitute debtor's earnings beyond basic needs must still go toward repayment.","The 'gracious lending' encouragement (leniency, waiver) discussed here should not be confused with the specific concept of Husnal Qadha (gracious REPAYMENT by the debtor above principal), covered in the next section."],"distinctions":["Majority view (Maliki/Shafi'e: no punishment for genuinely struggling debtors) vs Hanafi view (Abu Hanifa: temporary imprisonment then reassessment)"],"relatedConcepts":["Muflis (insolvency)","Penalty on default","Husnal Qadha"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.9","pages":[161,162]}}]},{"id":"t7.10","section":"7.10","title":"Instructions for the Creditor","pages":[162,162],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["creditor-duties"],"intuition":"What does Shari’ah expect of a lender dealing with a struggling debtor?","simpleExplanation":"Creditors are encouraged to give more time or waive part of the debt if the debtor is in difficulty (e.g. Ka‘ab waiving half of Abu Hadrad’s debt). Most jurists forbid punishing genuinely troubled debtors. A creditor cannot demand early payment of a debt with a settled date. But extra time is not the debtor’s right; surplus earnings must go to repayment.","academicExplanation":["The Qur’an encourages respite or waiver for debtors in difficulty; the Prophet encouraged politeness and partial waiver (Ka‘ab ibn Malik waived half of Abu Hadrad’s debt). Malikis and Shafi‘is do not allow punishment or arrest of genuinely troubled debtors; Abu Hanifa: imprison a non-payer for two to three months, then enquire and release if genuinely unable. A debt with a settled due date cannot be demanded early unless the debtor transgresses. If the creditor will not give time, he cannot be compelled; even a destitute debtor is not entitled to more time as a right; earnings beyond food needs go to repayment."],"examExplanation":"Encourage respite/waiver; no punishment of genuinely troubled debtors (Maliki, Shafi‘i); no early demand for dated debt; respite not a debtor’s right.","keyPoints":["Abu Hanifa: short imprisonment then enquiry."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Ka‘ab and Ibn Abi Hadrad","kind":"textbook","text":"When Ka‘ab pressed Ibn Abi Hadrad loudly in the mosque, the Prophet (pbuh) asked Ka‘ab to waive half, which he did, and told the debtor to pay the rest (p. 162)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t7.9","t7.13"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Can a creditor demand early repayment of a debt with a settled due date?","options":["Yes, any time","No, so long as the debtor does not transgress the terms","Only with interest","Only for Qard al Hasan"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 162."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Maliki and Shafi‘i","b":"Abu Hanifa","text":"The first do not allow arrest of a genuinely troubled debtor and recommend more time; Abu Hanifa would imprison a defaulter for two to three months, then release him if genuinely unable (p. 162)."}]},{"id":"t7.11","section":"7.11","title":"Husnal Qadha (Gracious Payment) and Current Accounts","pages":[162,163],"tier":"core","concepts":["husnal-qadha","current-account"],"intuition":"If paying back a little extra is Sunnah, why can’t banks do it systematically for current accounts?","simpleExplanation":"Repaying more than the principal without precondition is commendable (the Prophet repaid Jabir extra and gave a better camel). But it cannot be a system — that would make loans yield profit. Current accounts in Islamic banks are loans: guaranteed, no profit share, may carry service charges. Some boards allow occasional gifts; most do not favour it.","academicExplanation":["Repaying in excess without a precondition is commendable (the Prophet repaid Jabir more than owed; ordered a better camel given). But it is a matter of individual discretion; adopted as a system by banks or government it envisages explicit (customary rate) or implicit (expected return, e.g. nominal GNP growth) addition.","Current deposits in Islamic banks are loans repayable on call; they carry no weightage in profit allocation. The OIC Fiqh Council: the obligation to repay is unaffected by the bank’s solvency; such accounts are not eligible for profit as they bear no risk; the relationship is creditor–debtor. Banks may state in account forms that they will invest current funds at their discretion in compliant modes and may take service charges. Departure: some boards (e.g. Faysal Islamic Bank of Sudan) allow varied, non-regular prizes/gifts without prior knowledge; most boards do not favour it. Banks may pass a part of non-fund income to current depositors as a gift, provided no prior inducement and not a systematic return."],"examExplanation":"Husnal Qadha: extra without precondition commendable, but not as a system. Current accounts = Qard: guaranteed, no profit, service charges allowed; gifts only if no inducement/system (minority boards allow prizes).","keyPoints":["Current accounts carry no weightage.","Repayment unaffected by bank solvency."],"definitions":[{"term":"Husnal Qadha","definition":"Gracious repayment of a loan — giving more than the principal without any precondition."}],"examples":[{"title":"A better camel","kind":"textbook","text":"Lacking a camel of the age borrowed, the Prophet (pbuh) repaid with a better one: “the best of you are those who repay handsomely” — an unconditioned extra is commendable (pp. 162–163)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t8.5.2","t7.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the predominant ruling, current account holders in Islamic banks:","options":["Share profits by weightage","Are not eligible for profit as their deposits bear no risk","Receive fixed interest","Bear losses"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 163."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.11","sectionTitle":"Husnal Qadha; Remitting a Part of a Loan and Prepayment Rebate","title":"Husnal Qadha (Gracious Repayment) and Rules on Debt Remission/Prepayment Rebate","covers":["t7.11"],"overview":"Voluntary, unconditioned repayment in excess of principal (Husnal Qadha) is commendable but cannot be systematized; remission of part of a debt for early payment is permitted only for already-due debts (Duyun Halah), not for not-yet-due debts, and modern Murabaha rebates are contested.","simpleExplanation":"If a borrower voluntarily pays back more than they owed, with no prior agreement or expectation, that's a good and praiseworthy thing -- but it can't become a rule or system, because that would basically turn every loan into an interest-bearing one. Similarly, if you owe money that's already due and you offer to pay early, the lender CAN choose to let you pay less as a reward for paying early -- but if the money isn't due yet, discounting it for early payment is treated as interest. Banks today mostly avoid giving early-payment discounts on Murabaha as a routine practice, though they may still choose to give one at their own discretion.","academicExplanation":"Husnal Qadha (gracious repayment): repaying a loan in excess of the principal, without any precondition, is commendable and consistent with the Sunnah -- e.g. Jabir reports the Prophet repaid him more than the principal owed, and once ordered a better camel repaid since a camel of the exact borrowed age wasn't available. However, this must remain a matter of individual discretion, never systematized, because systematizing it would mean a loan necessarily yields profit -- turning implicit/explicit additions (a customary rate, or an investor's expectation of GNP-linked return) into a structural feature, which is impermissible. Current account deposits (treated as Qard, fully repayable on demand regardless of bank solvency, per OIC Fiqh Council resolution) are thus not eligible for a profit share, since they bear no risk; the depositor-bank relationship here is creditor-debtor. Some Shari'ah boards (e.g. Faysal Islamic Bank of Sudan) permit nonregular, varied gifts/prizes for such risk-free deposits (without prior inducement) to help mobilize funds and balance reward distribution, though most boards disfavor this; banks may also pass along part of non-fund service income (e.g. currency transfer fees) as a gift to current account holders, provided it's not systematized as a return. On remitting part of a loan/prepayment rebate (7.12): three Hadith traditions are examined. The Bani al-Nadhir tradition ('remit a part and take the rest earlier') is reconciled by jurists (via Waqidi's details) as referring specifically to remission of accrued Riba (not principal) owed to the Jews of Banu Nadhir. The Miqdad bin Aswad tradition (accepting 90 dirhams in place of 100 owed) was explicitly condemned by the Prophet as involving Riba for both parties. Imam Malik holds there is no difference of opinion on the illegality of remitting part of a Dayn for early payment of the rest -- equating it with increasing a debt for extending time, i.e. clear Riba. The third (Ka'ab/Abdullah bin Abi Hadrad) tradition, reconciled by Shah Waliullah, is distinguished as relating to an ALREADY-DUE debt (Dayn al-Halah), unlike the first two traditions (not-yet-due debt). The resolving distinction: Duyun Halah (already-due, demandable debts) -- remission for early payment IS allowed by nearly all jurists, since delay isn't the debtor's right in such cases, though it should not be made a precondition; Duyun Mu'ajjalah (debts with a settled future due date, not yet due) -- remission for early payment is NOT allowed (Imam Malik explicitly disapproves prepayment discounts here, citing Zaid b. Thabit and Abdullah b. Umar). Some later Hanafi jurists distinguish Musawamah-created debts from Murabaha-Mu'ajjal debts (where a profit margin was added specifically for the credit period): they allow remission of the remaining-period profit margin if the debtor pays early or dies, on grounds of mutual benefit. However, the majority of contemporary Shari'ah scholars (OIC Fiqh Academy, Middle Eastern Shari'ah committees) disallow such remission in bank Murabaha operations, viewing it as too similar to interest-based instalment techniques. AAOIFI Shari'ah Standards prohibit a CONTRACTUAL rebate commitment (since Murabaha price must be fixed once and for all), but permit the bank to give a rebate for early payment at its SOLE discretion, provided there was no prior commitment to do so.","examExplanation":"Husnal Qadha: voluntary, unconditioned excess repayment is commendable, but can NEVER be systematized (would make loans structurally profit-yielding). Duyun Halah (already-due debt) -- remission for early payment IS allowed (majority view), not as a precondition. Duyun Mu'ajjalah (not-yet-due, settled future date) -- remission for early payment is NOT allowed; treated as Riba (Imam Malik). Miqdad's 90-for-100 case: explicitly condemned as Riba by the Prophet. Modern Murabaha rebate: majority of contemporary scholars disallow CONTRACTUAL/systematic rebate commitments (too interest-like); AAOIFI permits a discretionary, non-contractual rebate given solely at the bank's choice.","keyPoints":["Husnal Qadha: voluntary, unconditioned repayment above principal is commendable (Sunnah-consistent) but must remain purely discretionary -- never systematized, since that would make loans structurally profit-yielding.","Current account deposits are treated as Qard, fully repayable on demand regardless of bank solvency, and are NOT eligible for a profit share (though some boards allow nonregular, uninduced gifts/prizes).","Duyun Halah (already-due, demandable debt): remission of part for early payment of the rest IS allowed by nearly all jurists (as long as not made a precondition) -- delay is not the debtor's right here.","Duyun Mu'ajjalah (debt with a settled future due date, not yet due): remission for early payment is NOT allowed -- treated as Riba (Imam Malik's clear position).","The Miqdad bin Aswad case (accepting 90 dirhams for a 100-dirham not-yet-due debt) was explicitly condemned by the Prophet as involving Riba for both parties.","Some later Hanafi jurists permit remitting the remaining-period profit margin in Murabaha-Mu'ajjal specifically (where the margin was tied to the credit period) on early payment or death -- but most contemporary scholars reject this for bank Murabaha as too interest-like.","AAOIFI: a CONTRACTUAL prepayment rebate commitment is prohibited (Murabaha price must be fixed once and for all), but a discretionary rebate, given solely at the bank's own choice with no prior commitment, is permitted."],"definitions":[{"term":"Husnal Qadha","definition":"Gracious payment of a loan/debt -- repaying in excess of the principal without a precondition; a matter of individual discretion, not a system."},{"term":"Duyun Halah","definition":"Debts that have become due or could be called back at any time (demandable debts)."},{"term":"Duyun Mu'ajjalah","definition":"Debts whose time of payment has been mutually settled between creditor and debtor, and which are not yet due."}],"conditions":["Remission of part of a debt for early payment is permitted only for Duyun Halah (already-due debts), and must not be made a precondition of the transaction.","A prepayment rebate in Murabaha must not be a contractual commitment (per AAOIFI); it may only be given at the bank's sole discretion, with no prior commitment."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Miqdad bin Aswad case (condemned)","kind":"textbook","text":"Miqdad accepted 90 dirhams in place of a 100-dirham debt not yet due, for early payment; when the Prophet learned of this, he said 'you got yourself and the other party involved in Riba.'"},{"title":"Current account gifts (contested)","kind":"textbook","text":"Some Shari'ah boards (e.g. Faysal Islamic Bank of Sudan) permit giving nonregular, uninduced prizes to current account holders (who bear no risk and get no profit share); most Shari'ah boards, however, disfavor this practice."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often conflate the Bani al-Nadhir and Miqdad traditions since both involve 'remitting part of a debt'; jurists reconcile them by noting the Bani al-Nadhir remission was of accrued RIBA (not principal), while Miqdad's case was a genuine early-payment discount on a not-yet-due debt, condemned as Riba.","The distinction between Duyun Halah (remission for early payment allowed) and Duyun Mu'ajjalah (remission not allowed) is easy to miss -- the deciding factor is whether the debt is already due, not merely whether early payment is offered."],"distinctions":["Duyun Halah (already-due debt, remission for early payment allowed) vs Duyun Mu'ajjalah (not-yet-due debt, remission for early payment = Riba)","AAOIFI's discretionary, non-contractual rebate (permitted) vs a contractually committed prepayment rebate (prohibited)"],"relatedConcepts":["Murabaha","Bai' Mu'ajjal","Current accounts","Riba"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163,164,165]}}],"debate":[{"issue":"Can current account holders receive gifts or prizes?","criticism":"Some Shari’ah boards (e.g. Faysal Islamic Bank of Sudan) permit varied, non-regular prizes for risk-free deposits to help mobilise funds.","response":"Most boards do not favour it; gracious extra payment cannot become a system, or loans would necessarily yield profit.","alternative":"Passing part of non-fund income as an unannounced gift without inducement.","takeaway":"Current accounts earn no profit; any gift must not be pre-arranged or systematic."},{"issue":"Can current accounts receive gifts?","criticism":"The Faysal Islamic Bank of Sudan’s board allowed irregular, varied prizes for risk-free current accounts to mobilise funds.","response":"Most Shari’ah boards do not favour it; current accounts are loans with no share in profit. A bank may pass part of non-fund service income as a gift only if there is no prior inducement and it does not become a system of return.","takeaway":"No systematic return on current accounts."}]},{"id":"t7.12","section":"7.12","title":"Remitting a Part of a Loan and Prepayment Rebate","pages":[163,165],"tier":"core","concepts":["prepayment-rebate","murabaha"],"intuition":"If a Murabaha client pays early, can the bank cut the price? Only as a discretionary rebate, not a contractual promise.","simpleExplanation":"Remitting part of a debt for early payment (“Dha‘awoo wa Ta‘ajjloo”) is allowed by almost all for debts already due, not for debts not yet due. Majority of contemporary scholars and the OIC Fiqh Academy do not allow contractual rebate for early payment in Murabaha. AAOIFI allows a rebate at the bank’s sole discretion if not committed in the contract.","academicExplanation":["Three traditions: “remit a part and take earlier” (Banu Nadhir’s expulsion); Miqdad accepting 90 for 100 — the Prophet called it Riba; and Ka‘ab waiving half of Abu Hadrad’s due debt. Jurists reconcile: the Banu Nadhir remission concerned accrued Riba (Waqidi: 120 dinars due, 80 principal accepted). Imam Malik: remitting part of a debt for early payment is like increasing a debt for more time — Riba without doubt.","Distinction: Duyun Halah (due/callable) — partial remission for early payment allowed by almost all, not as a condition; Duyun Mu’ajjalah (not yet due) — not allowed (Malik: not permissible to pay less before the due date). Shah Waliullah: the first two instances concerned debts not yet due; Ka‘ab’s was due. Some later Hanafis distinguish Murabaha–Mu’ajjal (where profit was charged for time) and require remission for the remaining period on early payment or death.","The majority of contemporary scholars, the OIC Fiqh Academy and Middle East Shari’ah committees do not allow contractual remission in bank Murabaha, as it resembles interest-based instalment techniques. AAOIFI prohibits contractual rebate (price fixed once) but allows a discretionary rebate if not committed; experts suggest referring each case to the Shari’ah advisor."],"examExplanation":"Partial remission for early payment: allowed for due debts (Duyun Halah), not for debts not yet due (Duyun Mu’ajjalah). Murabaha: no contractual rebate (OIC, majority); AAOIFI: discretionary rebate if not stipulated; Shari’ah advisor case by case.","keyPoints":["Miqdad’s 90-for-100: the Prophet called it Riba.","Later Hanafis’ minority view on Murabaha–Mu’ajjal."],"definitions":[{"term":"Duyun Halah","definition":"Debts that have become due or can be called back at any time."},{"term":"Duyun Mu’ajjalah","definition":"Debts whose payment time is settled and not yet due."},{"term":"Dha‘awoo wa Ta‘ajjloo","definition":"“Remit a part and take earlier” — tradition relating to Banu Nadhir, interpreted as remission of accrued Riba."}],"examples":[{"title":"Banu Nadhir reconciled","kind":"textbook","text":"“Remit a part and take it early” (Banu Nadhir) seems to conflict with Miqdad’s case, where remitting 10 of 100 for early payment was called Riba. Waqidi’s report shows the Banu Nadhir remission was of the Riba portion (120 dinars reduced to the 80 principal) (pp. 163–164)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t9.9.5","t7.10"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What is AAOIFI’s position on rebates for early payment in Murabaha?","options":["Mandatory rebate","Contractual rebate prohibited, but a discretionary rebate not committed in the contract is allowed","Always prohibited","Allowed only for governments"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 165."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Duyun Halah (due debts)","b":"Duyun Mu’ajjalah (not yet due)","text":"Remitting part of a debt already due for prompt payment is allowed by almost all jurists; a discount for paying before the due date is not (Malik, Shah Waliullah) (p. 164)."}]},{"id":"t7.13","section":"7.13","title":"Penalty on Default (and Insolvency of the Debtor)","pages":[165,167],"tier":"core","concepts":["late-payment-penalty","insolvency"],"intuition":"Without interest, what stops customers delaying payment? Penalty clauses paid to charity — and compensation for proven actual loss.","simpleExplanation":"Classical jurists preferred imprisonment over fines, and early Islamic banking boards disallowed automatic penalties. Now the OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI and Pakistan’s SAB approve contractual penalty clauses, with proceeds to charity. Banks may seek compensation for actual loss via courts/arbitration (not opportunity cost). Some boards allow compensation at the bank’s realised return during the delay. No penalty on Qard al Hasan. If the debtor is genuinely insolvent (Muflis), his assets are sold and distributed pro rata.","academicExplanation":["Classical jurists generally disfavoured pecuniary penalties, allowing harsh treatment or imprisonment; early Islamic bank boards did not allow automatic penalty clauses in Murabaha–Mu’ajjal lest they become interest. A wilful defaulter resembles a usurper (Ghasib) who must return profits on usurped property; hence scholars later allowed penalties. Default threatens banks, savers and economies — in instalment sales, Murabaha, leasing, PLS profit shares and Salam/Istisna‘a delivery.","Some classical and almost all contemporary scholars allow T‘azir as fines; some Malikis oblige a delaying debtor to pay for charity. The OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI and the SAB approve penalty clauses, balancing the problem with keeping the distinction between interest and Murabaha profit; penalties go to charity and cannot be the creditor’s income. Banks may claim liquidated damages decided by a court or independent committee based on actual loss (profit it could have earned on similar investment during delay per this view), proved by the bank — not conventional opportunity cost. Some boards (Al Baraka Sudan fatwa) allow compensation at the bank’s actual realised return during the delay, if harm is material and the debtor solvent and deliberately tardy; nothing if the bank earned nothing.","Penalties apply to Dayn, not Qard: for Qard al Hasan the creditor should give time — a penalty would be usury. OIC Fiqh Council: penalty clauses allowed in all financial debt contracts except Qard al Hasan; void if the client proves the failure was beyond control or the bank suffered no loss.","Insolvency (7.13.1): a Muflis’ fraudulent bankruptcy may be pressed and imprisoned; if genuinely unable, he is declared insolvent, assets sold and proceeds distributed pro rata, with time for the remainder; the State can help resolve. Malik and Shafi‘i allow arrest only if hidden wealth is possible. A seller finding his sold goods intact with the bankrupt has the first right (majority; Abu Hanifa: distribute among all creditors)."],"examExplanation":"Penalty clauses approved (OIC, AAOIFI, SAB) — proceeds to charity. Compensation only for actual loss via court/arbitration (no opportunity cost); some boards: realised return during delay if debtor solvent and tardy. No penalty on Qard al Hasan; void if beyond control or no loss. Muflis: assets sold, pro rata; seller’s first right to own goods (majority).","keyPoints":["Wilful defaulter likened to a usurper (Ghasib).","Penalty is not income.","Al Baraka Sudan: compensation at realised return, only if bank earned it.","Penalty void if default beyond client’s control.","A wilful defaulter is likened to a usurper (Ghasib), who must return any profit made on usurped property.","No penalty may be imposed for delay in repaying Qard al Hasan."],"definitions":[{"term":"T‘azir","definition":"Discretionary punishment — here, fines on wilful defaulters."},{"term":"Muflis","definition":"An insolvent/bankrupt debtor."},{"term":"Ghasib","definition":"Usurper; a wilful defaulter is likened to one."}],"examples":[{"title":"Al Baraka Sudan fatwa","kind":"textbook","text":"Compensation may be charged if the harm is actual, the client is solvent and deliberately late; measure it by the bank’s realised return during the delay. If the bank earned nothing in that period, it claims nothing (p. 166)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t3.3.1","t9.9.4","t17.4.4"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"In which case may a penalty clause NOT be imposed, per the OIC Fiqh Council?","options":["Murabaha","Ijarah","Qard al Hasan","Istisna‘a"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"A penalty on Qard al Hasan is usury (pp. 166–167)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.13","sectionTitle":"Penalty on Default; Insolvency of the Debtor","title":"Penalty on Default and Handling Insolvency (Muflis)","covers":["t7.13"],"overview":"Classical jurists preferred harsh treatment/imprisonment over pecuniary penalties, but contemporary Shari'ah bodies now permit penalty clauses (proceeds to charity) and actual-loss compensation for wilful default on Dayn (not Qard); insolvent debtors (Muflis) are protected from fraudulent-bankruptcy treatment but must still repay from asset liquidation.","simpleExplanation":"In the past, scholars preferred to punish a defaulting debtor personally (like imprisonment) rather than charge a cash penalty, since a cash penalty looked too much like interest. But because loan defaults have become such a big problem for banks, modern scholars now allow a penalty clause in contracts -- as long as the money collected goes to charity, not the bank's own profit, and it only applies to genuine debts from sales/leases (not pure loans). Banks can also claim real, provable losses through a court, but not made-up 'what we could have earned' numbers. If someone genuinely can't pay because they're broke (not because they're cheating), their assets are sold to pay creditors fairly, and they get more time for whatever's still owed.","academicExplanation":"Classical jurists generally disfavored pecuniary punishment for default, preferring harsh treatment or imprisonment; early Islamic banking religious boards similarly disallowed automatic penalty-fine clauses in Murabaha-Mu'ajjal agreements, to prevent Murabaha from becoming a vehicle for interest. The juristic analogy treats a wilful defaulter like a usurper (Ghasib), who must return any profit made on usurped property along with the property itself -- on this basis, scholars later permitted banks to impose penalties for client default, given the severity of the global nonperforming-loan problem threatening the Islamic banking system's viability. Classical and contemporary scholars now generally allow T'azir (discretionary punishment) in the form of fines for delaying borrowers; some Maliki jurists require the fine go to charitable activities. Major Shari'ah bodies (OIC Islamic Fiqh Council, AAOIFI, Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench) have approved penalty clauses in contracts, balancing the practical problem against Shari'ah principles that keep such fines distinct from interest and Murabaha profit -- the penalty must go to charity, never to the creditor's own income. Banks may separately claim liquidated damages/compensation for actual loss from default, with the amount determined by a court or independent reconciliation committee based on the profit the bank could have earned by investing the amount elsewhere during the delay -- but this 'actual loss' must be proven to the court's/arbitrator's satisfaction, and explicitly excludes conventional 'opportunity cost.' Some Shari'ah boards (e.g. Bank Al Baraka Sudan) permit charging the defaulter based on the bank's own realized Murabaha portfolio rate during the delay period specifically, conditioned on the client being prosperous yet deliberately tardy, and on the harm being material/actual (if the bank earned nothing during the period, it demands nothing). Critically, penalty imposition differs by contract type: permitted for Dayn (debt from sale/exchange, where deferral via dilatory tactics justifies a fine to charity plus possible actual-loss compensation), but NOT permitted for Qard (pure loan) -- here the creditor must simply give more time, since imposing a penalty on a pure loan is usury in the strict sense (per OIC Fiqh Council). The OIC Fiqh Council also resolved that a penalty clause becomes null and void if the client proves his failure was due to reasons beyond his control, or that the bank suffered no actual loss from the breach. On insolvency (7.13.1): a debtor genuinely unable to pay is termed Muflis (insolvent/bankrupt); care must be taken to rule out fraudulent bankruptcy (in which case he may be pressed/imprisoned), but a genuinely struggling debtor can be declared insolvent, with all assets sold and proceeds distributed pro rata among creditors; remaining unpaid debts require the debtor be given time for easement. Jurists differ on arresting an insolvent debtor -- Imam Malik and Imam Shafi'e permit arrest only if there's a possibility of hidden wealth. If a commodity sold on credit is still in the insolvent buyer's possession unchanged, the original seller has first right to reclaim it (per Hadith and majority view: 'if a man finds his same things with a bankrupt, he has more right to take them back than anyone else'), whereas Imam Abu Hanifa favors distributing proceeds among all creditors instead.","examExplanation":"Classical preference: harsh treatment/imprisonment over pecuniary penalty. Modern shift (due to NPL severity): penalty clauses now approved by OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI, Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench -- BUT penalty proceeds must go to CHARITY, never bank income. Separately, banks can claim liquidated damages/compensation for ACTUAL, PROVEN loss (not conventional opportunity cost), decided by court/arbitration. Key distinction: penalty/compensation permitted for Dayn (debt from sale/exchange) default; NOT permitted for Qard (pure loan) default -- pure loan default just requires more time from the creditor (penalty on Qard = usury). OIC: penalty clause voided if default was beyond debtor's control, or bank suffered no actual loss. Insolvency (Muflis): rule out fraud first; genuine insolvent -- assets sold, proceeds distributed pro rata, more time for remainder. If unpaid credit-sold goods are still with the insolvent buyer unchanged, original seller has first right to reclaim them (majority view; Abu Hanifa favors pro rata distribution instead).","keyPoints":["Classical preference was harsh treatment/imprisonment over pecuniary penalties; contemporary bodies (OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI, Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench) now approve penalty clauses due to the severity of the global nonperforming-loan problem.","A wilful defaulter is analogized to a usurper (Ghasib), who must return any profit made on the usurped property along with the property.","Penalty proceeds must go to charity -- never to the creditor's own income, to keep the penalty distinct from interest.","Banks may separately claim liquidated damages/compensation for actual, provable loss from default (via court/arbitration), explicitly excluding conventional 'opportunity cost.'","Penalty/compensation for default is permitted for Dayn (debt from sale/exchange transactions) but NOT for Qard (pure loan) -- imposing a penalty on a pure loan default is usury in the strict sense; the creditor must instead give more time.","A penalty clause becomes null and void if the client proves his failure was due to reasons beyond his control, or that the bank suffered no actual loss.","An insolvent debtor (Muflis) genuinely unable to pay: fraud must be ruled out first; if genuine, all assets are sold and proceeds distributed pro rata among creditors, with more time given for any remainder.","If unpaid, credit-sold goods remain unchanged in the insolvent buyer's possession, the original seller has first right to reclaim them (majority view); Imam Abu Hanifa instead favors pro rata distribution among all creditors."],"definitions":[{"term":"Muflis","definition":"An insolvent or bankrupt debtor who genuinely does not have enough money to pay his debt(s)."},{"term":"T'azir","definition":"Discretionary punishment (e.g. a fine) not expressly set by Qur'an/Sunnah, imposed at a judge's discretion."},{"term":"Ghasib","definition":"A usurper of another's property, who must return any profit made on the usurped property along with the property itself -- the analogy used for wilful defaulters."}],"conditions":["Penalty clauses are permissible only for Dayn (sale/exchange-created debt) default, not for Qard (pure loan) default.","Penalty proceeds must be given to charity, not retained as creditor income.","Liquidated damages/compensation must reflect the bank's actual, proven loss, not conventional 'opportunity cost,' and must be determined by a court or independent reconciliation/arbitration body.","A penalty clause is voided if the debtor proves the default was due to circumstances beyond his control, or that the bank suffered no actual loss."],"principles":[],"processSteps":["Confirm whether default is on Dayn (penalty/compensation may apply) or pure Qard (penalty not permitted; more time required instead).","If Dayn default is wilful, a penalty (fine) may be imposed, to be given to charity.","The bank may separately seek compensation for actual, proven loss via court or arbitration.","If the debtor proves the default was beyond his control, or that the bank suffered no loss, the penalty clause is voided."],"examples":[{"title":"Al Baraka Sudan's realized-rate compensation approach","kind":"textbook","text":"The bank calculates compensation based on the actual return it realized on its Murabaha portfolio during the specific delay period (e.g. 3 months); if the bank earned nothing during that period, it demands nothing from the client -- conditioned on the client being prosperous yet deliberately tardy."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often assume any default penalty is a form of disguised interest; the key distinguishing features that make it Shari'ah-compliant are that (a) it applies only to Dayn (not Qard), (b) the proceeds go to charity (not the bank), and (c) it's void if default was beyond the debtor's control.","Liquidated damages (actual, provable loss) are sometimes confused with 'opportunity cost' -- the chapter explicitly excludes conventional opportunity-cost calculations; the loss must be actual and proven."],"distinctions":["Penalty on Dayn default (permitted, proceeds to charity) vs penalty on Qard default (not permitted -- pure usury; more time required instead)","Actual, proven loss (compensable) vs conventional opportunity cost (not compensable)"],"relatedConcepts":["Qard al-Hasan","Murabaha","T'azir","Hawalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,166,167]}}],"subsections":[{"number":"7.13.1","title":"Insolvency of the Debtor","page":167,"points":["Fraudulent vs genuine bankruptcy","Assets sold, pro rata distribution","Seller’s first right to intact goods (majority)"]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Penalty (to charity)","b":"Compensation (to bank)","text":"Penalty is a deterrent paid to charity; compensation covers the bank’s proven actual loss, decided by a court or arbitrator, not opportunity cost."}]},{"id":"t7.14","section":"7.14","title":"Hawalah (Assignment of Debt)","pages":[167,168],"tier":"core","concepts":["hawalah"],"intuition":"Cheques, drafts and bills of exchange — what classical contract underlies them?","simpleExplanation":"Hawalah transfers a debt from one debtor to another, freeing the original debtor. It may be restricted (paid from the assignor’s property held by the assignee) or unrestricted. Cheques, drafts, pay orders and bills of exchange are forms of Hawalah. It is gratuitous, at face value, immediate, binding, and the debt returns to the assignor if the assignee dies or goes bankrupt (majority).","academicExplanation":["Hawalah literally means transfer; legally, an agreement by which a debtor is freed by another becoming responsible — replacing one debtor with another. Restricted Hawalah: the assignee pays from the assignor’s assets in his possession; unrestricted: the payer pays from his own funds with recourse to the assignor if paid on his order. It differs from transfer of a right (replacing a creditor). Hawalah travelled to Europe via Spain and Sicily in the 12th century; with Suftajah (a document for paying in another place through a second party), it formed the basis of the bill of exchange. Cheques, drafts, pay orders, remittances, promissory notes, bills of exchange, ODs and endorsements are forms of Hawalah.","Sale of debt (no recourse) is prohibited for Gharar and Riba; assignment (with recourse) is permissible. Hawalah takes effect immediately, not temporary or contingent, though payment may be deferred to a specified date. It is noncommutative: no remuneration for the service; assignment at nominal value with recourse. It is binding. Majority: the obligation returns to the assignor on bankruptcy or death of the assignee. Hadith: “if debt is transferred from your debtor to a trustworthy rich debtor, he should agree” — a recommendation (majority), implying mutual consent. Wakalah may also be used to pay a debt."],"examExplanation":"Hawalah: transfer of debt to a new debtor; restricted/unrestricted; basis of cheques, drafts, bills (with Suftajah). Gratuitous, at face value, immediate, binding, with recourse (debt returns to assignor on assignee’s bankruptcy/death). Contrast: sale of debt prohibited.","keyPoints":["No remuneration for Hawalah itself.","Suftajah: payment in another place via a second party.","Hawalah must take effect immediately; payment can still be deferred to the due date.","Majority: the obligation returns to the assignor if the assignee dies or becomes bankrupt."],"definitions":[{"term":"Hawalah","definition":"Assignment of debt: an agreement by which a debtor is freed from a debt by another becoming responsible for it."},{"term":"Suftajah","definition":"A document through which payment for purchased goods was made in another place through a second party."}],"examples":[{"title":"Hawalah to Europe","kind":"textbook","text":"Hawalah, together with Suftajah, formed the basis of the bill of exchange and was carried to Europe through Spain and Sicily during the 12th-century crusades (p. 168)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t6.10","t13.2","t5.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"If the assignee in a Hawalah becomes bankrupt, the majority view is that:","options":["The debt is extinguished","The obligation returns to the assignor","The creditor bears the loss","The State pays"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 168."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.14","sectionTitle":"Hawalah (Assignment of Debt)","title":"Hawalah: Assignment of Debt","covers":["t7.14"],"overview":"Hawalah is an agreement by which a debtor is freed from a debt by another party becoming responsible for it, distinct from and permissible unlike a prohibited sale of debt because it preserves recourse to the original debtor.","simpleExplanation":"Hawalah is like transferring a debt to someone else, similar to how a cheque or bank transfer works today -- the original debtor is released, and a new party takes over the responsibility to pay. What makes this different from (and allowed unlike) simply 'selling' a debt is that if the new payer can't or won't pay, you can still go back to the original debtor. Things like cheques, drafts, and promissory notes in modern banking are all forms of Hawalah.","academicExplanation":"Hawalah literally means transfer -- of something from one person to another, or from one situation to another. Legally, it's an agreement by which a debtor is freed from a debt by another becoming responsible for it, i.e. the responsibility shifts, replacing one debtor with another. Hawalah may be restricted (the assignee pays from the assignor's assets/property in the assignee's possession) or unrestricted (payment need not come from the assignor's assets; the assignee undertakes to pay from his own funds, with recourse to the assignor if payment was made by the assignor's order). Hawalah (transfer of debt, replacing one debtor with another) differs from a transfer of right (replacing one creditor with another). Muslim jurists developed and encouraged Hawalah; it was carried to Europe via Spain and Sicily during the 12th-century Crusades, and, together with al-Suftajah, formed the basis of the bill of exchange in Islamic commercial law. 'Hawalah' also denotes the transfer document itself -- functionally a promissory note or bill of exchange; modern products like cheques, drafts, pay orders, remittances, promissory notes, bills of exchange, overdrafts (ODs), and endorsements are all forms of Hawalah. The key legal distinction from the prohibited 'sale of debt': in Hawalah (assignment), there is recourse to the assignor/original debtor if the assignee fails to pay for any reason; in a pure 'sale of debt,' the purchaser has no such recourse, which introduces Gharar/Riba and renders it prohibited -- except precisely when structured as Hawalah. Hawalah must take effect immediately -- it cannot be suspended for a period, concluded temporarily, or made contingent on an unlikely future event, though the assignee's payment date itself can validly be deferred to a mutually specified date. Hawalah is a noncommutative contract -- the assignee cannot take any remuneration for the service; assignment is allowed only at the debt's nominal value, with recourse preserved. It is a binding contract, not subject to unilateral termination. Per the majority of jurists, the obligation to pay reverts to the assignor if the assignee becomes bankrupt or dies -- ensuring no debt obligation is ever simply left unpaid. The Prophet recommended accepting Hawalah when transferred to a trustworthy, wealthy debtor ('procrastination in paying debts by a wealthy man is injustice... if debt is transferred from your debtor to a trustworthy rich debtor, he should agree') -- held by the majority as a recommendation, implying Hawalah is valid when contracted by mutual consent between assignor and assignee. Wakalah (agency) is also permissible for debt payment -- a person may be appointed as agent to pay the debt on the debtor's behalf.","examExplanation":"Hawalah = assignment of debt (debtor is freed; a new party becomes responsible), distinct from transfer of RIGHT (replacing one creditor). Restricted Hawalah: assignee pays from assignor's assets in his possession. Unrestricted Hawalah: assignee pays from his own funds, with recourse to the assignor if he paid on the assignor's order. Key legal distinction from prohibited sale of debt: Hawalah preserves RECOURSE to the original debtor if the new debtor fails to pay; pure sale of debt has NO recourse (hence Gharar/Riba, prohibited). Hawalah must take immediate effect (not suspended/contingent), though the debt's PAYMENT DATE can be deferred. Noncommutative -- assignee gets no remuneration. Binding, not unilaterally terminable. If assignee dies/goes bankrupt, obligation reverts to the original assignor (majority view) -- no debt is ever left unpaid. Modern instruments (cheques, drafts, promissory notes, bills of exchange, ODs, endorsements) are all Hawalah forms.","keyPoints":["Hawalah: an agreement freeing a debtor from a debt by shifting responsibility to another party, who becomes the new debtor.","Restricted Hawalah: payment made from the assignor's assets/property in the assignee's possession. Unrestricted Hawalah: assignee pays from his own funds, with recourse to the assignor if paid on his order.","Hawalah (transfer of debt/debtor) differs from a transfer of right (replacing the creditor).","The key distinction between prohibited 'sale of debt' and permissible 'assignment of debt' (Hawalah): Hawalah preserves recourse to the original debtor if the new debtor defaults; pure sale of debt does not, hence its Gharar/Riba-based prohibition.","Hawalah must take effect immediately (not suspended, temporary, or contingent on an unlikely event), though the payment date of the transferred debt can validly be deferred to a mutually specified date.","Hawalah is a noncommutative contract -- the assignee cannot take remuneration; it is binding and not subject to unilateral termination.","If the assignee becomes bankrupt or dies, the obligation to pay reverts to the original assignor (majority view) -- ensuring the debt is never simply left unpaid.","Modern banking instruments -- cheques, drafts, pay orders, remittances, promissory notes, bills of exchange, overdrafts, endorsements -- are all forms of Hawalah.","Wakalah (agency) is separately permissible for debt payment -- a person can be appointed as an agent to pay a debt."],"definitions":[{"term":"Hawalah","definition":"Literally, transfer; legally, an agreement by which a debtor is freed from a debt by another becoming responsible for it -- a contract of assignment of debt."}],"conditions":["Hawalah must take effect immediately; it must not be suspended for a period, concluded on a temporary basis, or made contingent on an unlikely future event.","Hawalah is valid only when contracted by mutual consent between the assignor and the assignee.","The assignee cannot take remuneration for the Hawalah service, since it is a noncommutative contract."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Hawalah's role in bill-of-exchange history","kind":"textbook","text":"Together with al-Suftajah, Hawalah formed the basis of the bill of exchange in Islamic commercial law, and was carried to Europe via Spain and Sicily during the 12th-century Crusades."}],"commonConfusions":["Hawalah is often confused with a plain 'sale of debt'; the critical distinguishing feature is recourse -- Hawalah preserves the assignor's/original debtor's liability if the new debtor defaults, while a pure sale of debt does not (making it Gharar/Riba-laden and prohibited).","Students sometimes think Hawalah can be made effective from a future date like Ijarah or Kafalah; unlike those, Hawalah itself must take immediate effect -- only the ultimate PAYMENT date of the transferred debt may be deferred."],"distinctions":["Hawalah (transfer of debt/debtor, permissible, preserves recourse) vs sale of debt (no recourse, prohibited for Gharar/Riba)","Transfer of debt (Hawalah, replaces the debtor) vs transfer of right (replaces the creditor)"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al-Dayn","Kafalah","Wakalah","Suftajah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Original Debtor Owes a Debt","description":"A debtor owes a debt to a creditor -- the debt may have arisen from a loan (Qard/Dayn) or any other credit transaction. Under the ordinary rule, the debtor alone remains liable to that creditor."},{"step":"A Third Party Is Brought In as Assignee","description":"The debtor arranges for a third party (the assignee) to take over responsibility for the debt. In restricted Hawalah, the assignee is to pay from the debtor's own assets/property already in the assignee's possession; in unrestricted Hawalah, the assignee undertakes to pay from his own funds, with recourse back to the debtor only if he paid on the debtor's order."},{"step":"Assignment Is Contracted by Mutual Consent, Taking Immediate Effect","description":"Hawalah is valid when contracted by mutual consent between the debtor (assignor) and the assignee -- and, per the Prophet's recommendation to accept Hawalah to a trustworthy, wealthy debtor, the creditor is expected to agree. The assignment itself must take effect immediately: it cannot be suspended for a period, concluded temporarily, or made contingent on an unlikely future event, though the assignee's actual payment date can validly be deferred to a mutually specified date."},{"step":"The Assignee Becomes Solely Responsible, Discharging the Original Debtor","description":"Once Hawalah takes effect, the responsibility shifts: the original debtor is freed from the debt, and the assignee is now the party responsible for it -- this is what makes Hawalah a transfer of debt (replacing one debtor with another), distinct from a transfer of right (which would replace the creditor instead)."},{"step":"Creditor Collects from the New Party at the Debt's Original (Par) Value, with Recourse Preserved","description":"The creditor now looks to the assignee for payment, at the exact nominal/par value of the debt -- Hawalah is a noncommutative contract, so the assignee cannot take any remuneration for the service, and no discount or premium may attach to the amount assigned. Crucially, if the assignee fails to pay for any reason (or dies or becomes bankrupt), recourse reverts to the original debtor/assignor per the majority view, so the debt is never simply left unpaid. This preserved recourse is exactly what distinguishes permissible Hawalah from a prohibited 'sale of debt,' where the purchaser has no recourse to the seller and the transaction is voided by Gharar/Riba."}]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Restricted Hawalah","b":"Unrestricted Hawalah","text":"In restricted Hawalah the payer pays from the assignor’s property in his hands; in unrestricted Hawalah he pays from his own funds and has recourse to the assignor if he paid on the assignor’s order (p. 167)."}]},{"id":"t7.15","section":"7.15","title":"Security/Guarantee (Kafalah) and Pledge (Rihn)","pages":[168,172],"tier":"core","concepts":["kafalah","rihn"],"intuition":"What security can an Islamic bank take — and who bears the risk of a pledged asset?","simpleExplanation":"Kafalah: a third party becomes surety for a debt. Rihn: the debtor pledges an asset. The pledged asset remains the pledger’s — its gains and losses are his; the pledgee holds it in trust. The pledge cannot be ‘foreclosed’ (appropriated) but can be sold through the court or an irrevocable power of attorney, with any excess returned. A bank-pledgee may benefit in return for maintenance; excess income goes to the pledger.","academicExplanation":["A lender may demand security; the Prophet borrowed from a Jew against his iron breastplate. 2:283 shows a pledge is permissible, at home or journey, even with non-Muslims. Two forms: Kafalah (suretyship — a third party becomes surety for payment; scope known, no preconditions) and Rihn (pledge by the debtor), both based on consent; Rihn is also a trusteeship. The creditor may claim from debtor and surety; a surety who pays can recover from the debtor and may receive Zakat (Gharimeen). If the guarantor agrees to assume the debt, it becomes Hawalah. A delay to the debtor extends to the surety, not vice versa. Surety of a surety is lawful; joint sureties are each liable for their share, successive sureties each for the whole. Goods held in trust (pledged to him, or leased) cannot be offered as guarantee.","Bank securities: letters of guarantee, post-dated cheques, promissory notes, frozen deposits, third-party guarantees, Hamish Jiddiyah and ‘Arbun. Whatever can be sold can be pledged, encumbered to the extent of the debt; a share in joint property can be pledged.","Risk and reward (7.15.1): “Pledge cannot be foreclosed; its Ghunm (accession) is for the pledger and its Ghurm (loss) upon him.” If destroyed without the pledgee’s negligence, the loss is the pledger’s; the pledgee recovers the loan. The pledge covers only the debt; excess belongs to the pledger. ‘Cannot be foreclosed’ means it must remain redeemable, not wrongfully appropriated; a clause that it passes to the pledgee in place of debt is invalid. On default the pledgee applies to court to sell, or holds an irrevocable power of attorney to sell and remit any excess.","Benefits (7.15.2): a pledged animal may be ridden and milked in return for its maintenance. Views: some Hanafis — no benefit even with permission (Riba); most Hanafis — with permission if not stipulated; Shafi‘i — benefit is the pledger’s; Maliki — pledger’s, but the pledgee may benefit if the debt arose from sale (not Qard), stipulated and time-bound; Hanbali — with permission. No condition in the loan contract giving the pledgee benefit is valid; extra income over expenses should go to the pledger. So a bank as pledgee may benefit for maintenance (e.g. a house), charge a customary service rate or lease it on, passing excess rental to the pledger. Possessory lien is permissible by analogy with a cash seller; mortgage without possession is allowed by contemporary scholars by analogy."],"examExplanation":"Kafalah (third-party surety) vs Rihn (debtor’s pledge, held in trust). Pledger bears risk and reward (Ghunm/Ghurm); pledge covers debt only; no foreclosure-appropriation, sale via court/power of attorney with excess returned. Pledgee benefit only for maintenance; excess to pledger. Lien and mortgage allowed.","keyPoints":["Surety who pays may receive Zakat as Gharim.","Successive sureties each liable for the whole.","Clause transferring the pledge in lieu of debt is invalid.","No condition in a Qard giving pledgee benefit.","Acceptable bank securities: guarantees, post-dated cheques, promissory notes, frozen deposits, third-party guarantees, Hamish Jiddiyah and ‘Arbun.","A surety who has to pay may receive Zakat (Gharimeen)."],"definitions":[{"term":"Kafalah","definition":"Suretyship: a third party becomes surety for payment of a debt or liability."},{"term":"Rihn","definition":"Pledge: the debtor hands over property as security; the creditor holds it as a trust."},{"term":"Possessory lien","definition":"Right to retain another’s property until a debt due from him is paid."}],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet’s breastplate","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet (pbuh) borrowed from a Jew against the security of an iron breastplate, still pledged at his passing — pledges are allowed at home and between Muslims and non-Muslims (pp. 168–169)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t14.6","t7.14","t5.6.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"A pledged asset is destroyed without any negligence by the pledgee. Whose loss is it?","options":["The pledgee’s","The pledger’s (debtor’s)","Shared equally","The surety’s"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 170."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.15","sectionTitle":"Security/Guarantee (Kafalah) in Loans; Risk and Reward in Pledge; Benefits from Pledge","title":"Kafalah (Suretyship) and Rihn (Pledge): Guarantee Mechanisms for Loans","covers":["t7.15"],"overview":"Kafalah (third-party suretyship) and Rihn (pledge/collateral) are the two Shari'ah-sanctioned guarantee mechanisms for loans; the pledger bears the risk and reward of the pledged property, and jurists differ on who may benefit from a pledge while it is held.","simpleExplanation":"If you lend money, you're allowed to ask for some kind of security. There are two main types: Kafalah, where a third person agrees to step in and pay if the original borrower doesn't, and Rihn, where the borrower hands over an item as collateral. Whoever owns the pledged item still owns its ups and downs in value (they benefit if it appreciates, they bear the loss if it's damaged without the lender's fault) -- the lender is just holding it safely, like a trustee, not owning it. Scholars disagree on whether the lender can actually use or benefit from the pledged item (like riding a pledged animal) while holding it as security.","academicExplanation":"A lender may demand security to which he has recourse if the borrower fails to repay; the Prophet himself borrowed from a Jew against the security of an iron breastplate. Qur'an 2:283 sanctions Rihn Maqbudah (a pledge with possession) especially when a scribe isn't available (e.g. while traveling), establishing that pledges are permissible regardless of travel status and even between a Muslim and non-Muslim. Guarantee comes in two forms: Kafalah (suretyship) and Rihn (pledge) -- both pre-Islamic contracts approved and later elaborated by Muslim jurists to protect creditors from iniquity. Kafalah literally means taking responsibility for a debt's payment or a person's court appearance; legally, a third party becomes surety for a debt unpaid by the originally liable person -- the scope of suretyship must be known and free of preconditions. Rihn (pledge) is a separate security device: the debtor hands over property as a pledge; unlike Kafalah (third-party liability), Rihn additionally functions as a trusteeship, with the creditor holding the pledged property as a trust. A creditor may also demand personal surety from a third party, creating additional liability -- the creditor may claim from either the debtor or the surety, and if the surety pays, the debtor must reimburse the surety; if the debtor cannot pay, the surety must pay the creditor (and, in doing so, may then be entitled to Zakat/charity as a Gharmeen). If a guarantor agrees to remit the principal debtor's debt himself, the effect is equivalent to Hawalah. A delay granted to the principal debtor extends to the surety too, but a delay given to the surety does NOT extend to the principal debtor. A surety agreement becomes enforceable upon the offer of surety, with the claimant's agreement; it is lawful to be surety for a surety; multiple sureties for one obligation (joint surety) each bear only their own share, but where multiple people become sureties one after another for the same debt, each is liable for the WHOLE debt (as are mutually surety jointly-indebted parties). A guarantee is ineffective over goods already held in trust by the principal debtor (e.g. pledged or leased items he holds cannot be furnished as guarantee). Bank guarantee tools include letters of guarantee, post-dated cheques, promissory notes, frozen cash deposits, third-party guarantees, Hamish Jiddiyah, and 'Arbun. Whatever is a valid subject of sale can be pledged (Rihn), encumbered to the extent of the debt; a share in jointly-owned property can also be pledged. On risk and reward in pledge (7.15.1): the pledger (debtor) remains the OWNER of the pledged property's risk and reward -- 'Pledge cannot be foreclosed, and it is from the pledger and for him is its Ghunm [accession/gain] and upon him is its Ghurm [loss]' (Hadith). If the pledge is lost/destroyed without the pledgee's proven negligence, the loss falls on the pledger (the pledgee, being a trustee, isn't liable and can still recover the loaned amount from the pledger). Any excess value above the loan amount belongs to the pledger. 'Pledge cannot be foreclosed' means the pledgee cannot simply appropriate the item on default (as was pre-Islamic practice) -- the pledge remains redeemable; on the debt's maturity if unpaid, the pledgee applies to the court to have the item sold and the debt recovered from proceeds (creditors may hold an irrevocable power of attorney to streamline this). On benefits from pledge (7.15.2): a Hadith permits a pledgee to ride a pledged animal and consume its milk in return for maintaining it, without needing the pledger's permission -- but jurists differ significantly on who may benefit from a pledge: some Hanafis hold the pledgee may NEVER benefit (even with permission), as it amounts to Riba; the majority of Hanafis allow pledgee benefit WITH the pledger's permission, provided it isn't stipulated at contract time; Shafi'e school holds it's the PLEDGER's right to benefit (item stays with the pledgee except when the pledger uses it); Maliki school allows pledgee benefit only if (i) the underlying obligation arose from a sale (not Qard), (ii) the benefit was stipulated at contract time, and (iii) its period is specified; Hanbali jurists allow pledgee use subject to the pledger's permission. The unifying theme: benefit should correspond to maintenance expenditure (else it's Riba), though no strict formula is mandated (the Prophet didn't specify equating expenses to benefit precisely); any income beyond expenses should go to the pledger. By analogy, an Islamic bank as pledgee may benefit from a pledge (e.g. a house) in return for its maintenance, charge the pledger a customary service rate, or lease it out -- rental beyond the customary service rate goes to the pledger. Beyond pledge, an Islamic bank has a right of possessory lien -- retaining another's property until a debt is paid -- permissible by analogy to a cash-sale seller's right to retain sold property until price payment. Mortgage (where only the property interest, not possession, transfers to the mortgagee) isn't discussed in traditional books but is permitted by contemporary scholars via analogy.","examExplanation":"Two guarantee mechanisms: Kafalah (third-party suretyship for debt/liability) and Rihn (pledge/collateral held in trust). Kafalah: surety must have known scope, no preconditions; multiple simultaneous sureties each owe only their share, but sequential/successive sureties each owe the WHOLE debt; guarantor remitting the debt himself = effectively Hawalah; delay to principal debtor extends to surety, but NOT vice versa. Rihn: pledger retains ownership's risk/reward (Ghunm/Ghurm) -- pledgee (trustee) not liable for non-negligent loss, but also can't appropriate the pledge on default ('pledge cannot be foreclosed') -- must go through court sale on maturity. Benefit from pledge: schools differ (some Hanafis: never; majority Hanafi: with pledger's permission, unstipulated; Shafi'e: pledger's right; Maliki: pledgee's benefit OK only if debt arose from sale (not Qard) + stipulated + time-bound; Hanbali: with permission) -- unifying idea: benefit should roughly match maintenance cost, excess goes to pledger. Bank guarantee tools: letters of guarantee, post-dated cheques, promissory notes, frozen deposits, third-party guarantees, Hamish Jiddiyah, 'Arbun. Possessory lien and mortgage also discussed (mortgage permitted by modern analogy, not traditional texts).","keyPoints":["Two Shari'ah guarantee mechanisms: Kafalah (third-party suretyship for an unpaid debt) and Rihn (pledge/collateral, held by the creditor as a trust).","Kafalah: suretyship scope must be known and precondition-free; simultaneous joint sureties each bear only their own share, but successive sureties (one after another) each bear liability for the WHOLE debt.","A delay granted to the principal debtor extends to the surety, but a delay given to the surety does NOT extend to the principal debtor.","The pledger (debtor) retains ownership of the pledged property's risk and reward (Ghunm/Ghurm) -- if lost/destroyed without the pledgee's proven negligence, the loss is the pledger's, not the trustee-pledgee's.","'Pledge cannot be foreclosed': the pledgee cannot simply appropriate the pledged item on default; on maturity if unpaid, the pledgee must go through court process to have it sold and the debt recovered from proceeds.","Jurists significantly differ on whether/how a pledgee may benefit from a pledge (e.g. riding a pledged animal) -- ranging from never permissible (some Hanafis) to permissible only with the pledger's stipulated, time-bound consent tied to a sale-created (not Qard-created) debt (Maliki).","The unifying principle across views: any pledgee benefit should roughly correspond to maintenance expenditure (else it risks becoming Riba); any excess income should go to the pledger.","An Islamic bank as pledgee may benefit from a pledge (e.g. a house) in return for maintenance, charge a customary service rate, or lease it out -- with rental beyond the customary rate going to the pledger.","Islamic banks also have a permissible right of possessory lien (retaining another's property until debt payment), by analogy to a cash-sale seller's retention right; mortgage (interest transferred, not possession) is permitted by contemporary scholars via analogy, though not discussed in traditional texts."],"definitions":[{"term":"Kafalah","definition":"Literally, taking responsibility for payment of a debt or a person's court appearance; legally, a third party becomes surety for a debt unpaid by the person originally liable."},{"term":"Rihn","definition":"Pledge or collateral -- to pledge or lodge a real/corporeal property of material value as security for a debt, enabling the creditor to recover the debt (by selling the pledged property) if unpaid."},{"term":"Ghunm / Ghurm","definition":"Accession/gain (Ghunm) and loss (Ghurm) -- per the Hadith on pledge, both belong to the pledger, not the pledgee, since the pledger remains the owner."}],"conditions":["The scope of a Kafalah suretyship must be known and free of preconditions.","A pledgee's benefit from a pledge, where permitted, generally should not be stipulated as a condition at the time of the loan contract (majority Hanafi view), or must meet specific conditions per school (e.g. Maliki: sale-created debt, stipulated, time-bound).","On default, the pledgee cannot simply appropriate the pledged item; recovery must go through a court-sanctioned sale process."],"principles":["Pledge cannot be foreclosed -- Ghunm (gain) is from the pledger and for him, Ghurm (loss) is upon him, since he remains the owner."],"processSteps":["Creditor requests security (Kafalah suretyship and/or Rihn pledge) at loan/credit origination.","If the debtor defaults and the debt matures unpaid, the pledgee applies to the court to have the pledged item sold.","Proceeds from the sale are used to recover the debt; any excess is returned to the pledger."],"examples":[{"title":"The Prophet's pledge to a Jewish creditor","kind":"textbook","text":"The Prophet borrowed from a Jew against the security of an iron breastplate, which was still with the creditor at the time of the Prophet's demise -- illustrating the permissibility of pledges even in a cross-faith transaction."},{"title":"Bank benefiting from a pledged house","kind":"textbook","text":"By analogy to a pledged animal's permissible use (riding/milk in return for maintenance), an Islamic bank holding a house as pledge may benefit from it in return for its maintenance, charge a customary service rate, or lease it out -- with rental beyond the customary rate going to the pledger."}],"commonConfusions":["Students often think a pledgee (creditor) can simply keep or sell the pledged item on default without any process; the Hadith 'pledge cannot be foreclosed' specifically rules this out -- a court-sanctioned sale process is required.","The rule on successive sureties (each liable for the WHOLE debt) versus simultaneous joint sureties (each liable only for their OWN share) is a frequently confused distinction."],"distinctions":["Kafalah (third-party personal suretyship) vs Rihn (pledge of a specific asset as collateral, held in trust)","Simultaneous joint sureties (each liable for own share) vs successive/sequential sureties (each liable for the whole debt)"],"relatedConcepts":["Hawalah","Hamish Jiddiyah","'Arbun","Zakat (Gharmeen)"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,169,170,171,172]},"transactionSteps":[{"step":"Kafalah 1: Principal Debtor's Obligation Exists","description":"A principal debtor owes a debt, fine, or other liability to a creditor. The creditor, wanting recourse beyond the debtor alone, calls for a guarantee -- one of the bank's tools being letters of guarantee, post-dated cheques, promissory notes, frozen cash deposits, or a third-party guarantee."},{"step":"Kafalah 2: Guarantor Voluntarily Undertakes Suretyship","description":"A third party (the Kafil/guarantor) becomes surety for the debt, with the scope of the suretyship known and free of preconditions. This does not release the principal debtor -- both remain liable side by side, unless the guarantor separately agrees to remit the debt himself, in which case the effect becomes equivalent to Hawalah (transference of debt) rather than Kafalah."},{"step":"Kafalah 3: Creditor May Claim from Either Party","description":"The creditor has the right to demand payment from either the principal debtor or the surety. A guarantee is ineffective, however, over goods already held in trust by the principal debtor (e.g. items he holds under pledge or lease cannot be furnished as his guarantee), and a delay granted to the principal debtor extends to the surety too -- though a delay granted only to the surety does not extend to the principal debtor."},{"step":"Kafalah 4: Guarantor's Right of Recourse If He Pays","description":"If the debtor cannot pay and the surety is obliged to pay the creditor instead, the principal debtor is bound to reimburse the surety -- and if the debtor still cannot pay, the surety who paid on his behalf may himself become eligible to receive Zakat/charity as a Gharmeen (one obliged to pay another's debt)."},{"step":"Rihn 1: Debtor Hands Over Property as Pledge","description":"Separately (or in combination), the debtor hands over property he owns -- whatever is a valid subject of sale, including a share in jointly-owned property -- as a Rihn (pledge), which the creditor (pledgee) then holds as a trust, encumbered only to the extent of the debt."},{"step":"Rihn 2: Pledger Retains Risk and Reward Throughout","description":"Ownership of the pledged property, and with it its risk and reward (Ghunm/Ghurm), stays with the pledger throughout -- per the Hadith 'pledge cannot be foreclosed, and it is from the pledger and for him is its Ghunm and upon him is its Ghurm.' If the pledge is lost or destroyed without the pledgee's proven negligence, the loss falls on the pledger, not the trustee-pledgee, who can still recover the amount lent from him."},{"step":"Rihn 3: On Default, Forfeiture Is Barred -- Only a Court Sale Can Recover the Debt","description":"'Pledge cannot be foreclosed' means the pledgee may never simply appropriate the pledged item for himself on default -- a contract provision to that effect is invalid. Instead, once the debt matures unpaid, the pledgee must apply to the court to have the pledged item sold (creditors may hold an irrevocable power of attorney to streamline this), with the proceeds used to recover the debt and any excess returned to the pledger."}]}],"subsections":[{"number":"7.15.1","title":"Risk and Reward in Pledge","page":170,"points":["Ghunm and Ghurm with pledger","Covers debt only","Sale via court or power of attorney"]},{"number":"7.15.2","title":"Benefits from Pledge","page":171,"points":["Benefit in return for maintenance","School views","Excess to pledger; lien and mortgage"]}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Kafalah","b":"Rihn","text":"Kafalah involves a third-party surety; Rihn involves the debtor’s own asset pledged as security."}],"table":{"caption":"Who may benefit from a pledge? (pp. 171–172)","head":["School","View"],"rows":[["Hanafi (majority of them)","Pledgee may benefit with the pledger’s permission if not stipulated in the contract; some Hanafis forbid it entirely"],["Shafi‘i","The pledger, as owner, has the right to benefit"],["Maliki","Pledgee may benefit if the debt is from a sale (not Qard), benefit is stipulated and its period specified"],["Hanbali","Pledgee may use it with the pledger’s permission"]]}},{"id":"t7.16","section":"7.16","title":"Bai‘ al Dayn (Sale of Debt/Debt Instruments)","pages":[172,172],"tier":"core","concepts":["bai-dayn","sukuk"],"intuition":"Malaysian practice allowed trading debt-based Sukuk at a discount. What is the wider view?","simpleExplanation":"Some Malaysian experts allow Bai‘ al Dayn at a discount (citing the Shafi‘i school), but the Jamhoor Ulama, the overwhelming majority of contemporary scholars and the OIC Fiqh Council (unanimously, including Malaysia) prohibit it; Shafi‘i jurists allowed it only at par.","academicExplanation":["Secondary trading of debt-based securities via Bai‘ al Dayn occurs in some Malaysian Sukuk; the Jamhoor do not accept it even if debts are asset-supported. Traditional jurists unanimously disallow sale of debt at discount or premium, as do most contemporary scholars. Malaysian experts cite the Shafi‘i school but overlook that Shafi‘is allowed it only at par. Rosly and Sanusi: trading Islamic bonds at discount via Bai‘ al Dayn is unacceptable to the Jumhur including al-Shafi‘i. The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council, with representation of all Islamic countries including Malaysia, unanimously approved the prohibition."],"examExplanation":"Bai‘ al Dayn at discount/premium prohibited (Jamhoor, OIC unanimously); Malaysian minority practice relies on Shafi‘i view which allowed only par value.","keyPoints":["OIC decision unanimous, including Malaysia."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Discounting a debt certificate","kind":"textbook","text":"An investor buys a 100,000 Murabaha receivable for 94,000 through Bai‘ al Dayn. The jurists’ majority and the OIC Fiqh Council reject this. The Shafi‘i allowance that Malaysian experts cite applied only to sale at par (p. 172)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The Shafi‘i school allowed discounted sale of debt, so Malaysian Bai‘ al Dayn is widely accepted.","right":"Shafi‘is allowed sale of debt only at par; the majority, including Middle-East jurists and the OIC Fiqh Council unanimously, reject discounted Bai‘ al Dayn (p. 172)."}],"relatedTopics":["t6.10","t15.3.6"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"How did the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council decide on Bai‘ al Dayn?","options":["Allowed at discount","Prohibited unanimously","Allowed for Sukuk","Left to each country"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 172."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.16","sectionTitle":"Bai' al-Dayn (Sale of Debt/Debt Instruments)","title":"Bai' al-Dayn: The Prohibition on Selling Debt at a Discount or Premium","covers":["t7.16"],"overview":"Traditional and the overwhelming majority of contemporary jurists, including the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council (with Malaysian representation), unanimously prohibit selling debt instruments at other than par value, contrary to some Malaysian Shari'ah practice for Sukuk secondary trading.","simpleExplanation":"Some markets (particularly in Malaysia) allow trading debt-based Islamic bonds at a discount or premium in secondary markets. But most scholars worldwide, and even the international body representing all Islamic countries (including Malaysia), say this isn't allowed -- you can't sell a debt for more or less than its exact face value, even if it's technically backed by real assets.","academicExplanation":"Secondary market trading of debt and debt-based securities is technically possible through Bai' al-Dayn, as seen with a variety of Malaysia-based Sukuk. However, Jamhoor Ulama (the mainstream body of jurists) reject this, even where the underlying Sukuk debt is asset-supported. Traditional Muslim jurists are unanimous that Bai' al-Dayn at a discount or premium (rather than par/face value) is not allowed in Shari'ah, and the overwhelming majority of contemporary Shari'ah scholars share this view. Some Malaysian experts have permitted this kind of sale, typically citing the Shafi'e school's ruling -- but this citation is misapplied, since Shafi'e jurists in fact allowed debt sale only where the debt was sold AT its par value, not at a discount or premium. Rosly and Sanusi (1999) note that trading Islamic bonds at a discount via Bai' al-Dayn has been found unacceptable by the Jumhur Ulama, including al-Shafi'e himself, and that Malaysian Islamic bonds' position remains unacceptable to Middle Eastern jurists even though some Malaysian jurists hold otherwise. The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council -- with representation from all Islamic countries, including Malaysia -- has approved the prohibition of Bai' al-Dayn unanimously, without a single dissent.","examExplanation":"Bai' al-Dayn = sale of debt at other than par (face) value -- unanimously prohibited by traditional jurists and the overwhelming majority of contemporary scholars, including the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council (unanimous, despite Malaysian representation). Malaysian practice permits discounted/premium trading of debt-based Sukuk in secondary markets, citing the Shafi'e school -- but this is a MISCITATION, since Shafi'e jurists allowed debt sale only AT par value, never at a discount/premium. Key exam point: even asset-backed Sukuk debt cannot be traded at a discount/premium per the mainstream (Jamhoor Ulama) position -- this differs from Chapter 6's point that genuinely asset-backed Sukuk are tradeable (that referred to equity/ownership-type Sukuk, not debt-representing ones traded at non-par prices).","keyPoints":["Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt at other than par/face value) is unanimously prohibited by traditional jurists and the overwhelming majority of contemporary scholars.","Some Malaysian experts permit discounted/premium secondary trading of debt-based Sukuk, but the mainstream (Jamhoor Ulama) rejects this, even for asset-supported debt.","The Shafi'e-school citation used to justify Malaysian practice is a misapplication -- Shafi'e jurists allowed debt sale only AT par value, not at a discount or premium.","Rosly and Sanusi's study confirms Malaysian Islamic bonds' Bai' al-Dayn structure remains unacceptable to Middle Eastern jurists, though some Malaysian jurists disagree.","The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council -- representing all Islamic countries including Malaysia -- unanimously approved the prohibition of Bai' al-Dayn, without a single dissent."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["Even where the Shafi'e school permits sale of a debt, it is allowed only at the debt's par (face) value, never at a discount or premium."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Students may think the OIC Fiqh Council's ruling reflects only Middle Eastern jurists' views against Malaysian practice; the chapter is explicit that the OIC Council itself has Malaysian representation and still approved the prohibition unanimously, without dissent -- this is a genuinely global consensus position, not a regional dispute.","This prohibition should not be confused with Chapter 6's point that genuinely asset-backed Sukuk (representing real ownership) are tradeable -- here the issue is specifically selling DEBT itself at non-par prices, which remains prohibited regardless of asset backing."],"distinctions":["Sale of debt at par value (permitted per Shafi'e view) vs sale of debt at a discount or premium (unanimously prohibited)"],"relatedConcepts":["Bai' al-Dayn (Chapter 6)","Sukuk","Al-Kali bil-Kali","Hawalah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.16","pages":[172]}}],"debate":[{"issue":"Can debt-based Sukuk be traded at a discount?","criticism":"Some Malaysian experts allow Bai‘ al Dayn, citing the Shafi‘i school.","response":"Shafi‘i jurists allowed debt sale only at par; the Jamhoor, most contemporary scholars and the OIC Fiqh Council unanimously prohibit it.","alternative":"Malaysian minority practice.","takeaway":"Debt instruments cannot be sold at a price other than face value."}]},{"id":"t7.17","section":"7.17","title":"Impact of Inflation on Loans/Debts","pages":[172,174],"tier":"core","concepts":["indexation"],"intuition":"Rs.100 lent today may buy less next year. Can the lender ask for Rs.120?","simpleExplanation":"Indexation of loans/debts to any currency, basket or gold is rejected by Shari’ah scholars, courts and boards (FSC Pakistan 1991: indexed Rs.100 → Rs.120 is Riba Al-Nasiah and Al-Fadl). The same units must be returned regardless of value changes. One may lend in gold or a stable currency, but the liability cannot increase. OIC (1993): parties may agree on the settlement day to pay in another currency at that day’s rate.","academicExplanation":["Scholars and courts have not accepted indexing loans/debts to currencies or gold. The FSC (14 November 1991, paras 153–234) reviewed Hidaya, Al-Mabsut, Bada‘i, Al Jaziri and others: indexing Rs.100 to Rs.120 is Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl. Al Jaziri: loans require equality — return the same quantity whatever the price change. Kasani: lending depreciated coins on condition of repayment in good coins is illegal; any stipulated benefit is Riba. Ibn Qudama: repay the same coins irrespective of increase or decrease in value. Justice Khalilur Rahman (SAB): debts settled equally in units; alternatives are Bai‘ Mu’ajjal or partnership, where the margin or realised profit accommodates concerns. Al Jaziri’s example: 4 lb of meat bought on credit at 5 qirsh (20 total) — still 20 if meat falls to 2.","Lending is gratuitous; loss of value to inflation is a greater virtue. The OIC Fiqh Council forbids indexation to cost of living, interest rates, GNP growth, gold or commodities; one may lend in terms of gold or a stable currency without the liability increasing. Depreciation affects money whether lent or kept; indexing draws benefit from the loan. OIC 8th session (1993): on the settlement day (not before), parties may agree to settle in another currency at that day’s rate; instalments likewise; at contracting, parties may agree to settle a deferred price/salary in various currencies or a given amount of gold; a debt in a specific currency should not be recorded in its gold/other-currency counter value."],"examExplanation":"No indexation (FSC 1991; OIC). Return same units. Lend in gold/stable currency allowed without increase. OIC 1993: settle in another currency only by agreement on settlement day at that day’s rate.","keyPoints":["Indexed Rs.100 → Rs.120 = Riba Al-Nasiah and Al-Fadl (FSC).","Meat example: 20 qirsh remains 20.","Settlement-day currency conversion allowed at prevailing rate."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Meat bought on credit (Al Jaziri)","kind":"textbook","text":"A buys 4 pounds of meat on credit at 5 qirsh per pound (20 qirsh). If the price falls to 2 qirsh per pound at payment, A still pays 20 qirsh, not 8."},{"title":"Meat bought on credit","kind":"textbook","text":"A buys 4 pounds of meat on credit at 5 qirsh a pound (20 qirsh). If the price falls to 2 qirsh at payment, A still pays 20 — not 8 (Al Jaziri, p. 174)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t4.7.4","t3.2.1.c"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under the OIC Fiqh Council (1993), when may parties agree to settle a debt in a currency other than the one specified?","options":["At the time of contract, at that day’s rate","On the day of settlement, at the rate prevailing on that date","Never","At any time at an inflation-adjusted rate"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 174."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch7-t11"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch7-t11","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"7.17","sectionTitle":"Impact of Inflation on Loans/Debts","title":"Inflation and Loans: Why Indexation Is Not Permitted","covers":["t7.17"],"overview":"Shari'ah scholars, courts, and Islamic bank boards reject indexing loans/debts to currency baskets, gold, or price levels; a fungible loan must be repaid in exactly the same nominal units borrowed, regardless of inflation or deflation.","simpleExplanation":"If you borrow $100, you pay back exactly $100 -- even if inflation means that $100 buys much less by the time you repay, or even if it would buy more. Adjusting the loan amount to 'keep pace' with inflation (indexation) is treated as a form of interest, because the extra amount charged has no genuine trade-based justification. The only real Islamic solution for someone worried about inflation is to not lend at all and instead do a genuine trade or partnership deal, where compensation flows through profit margins or shared risk, not a preset inflation formula.","academicExplanation":"Shari'ah scholars, courts, and Islamic bank Shari'ah committees/boards have not accepted indexing loans/debts to any currency/basket of currencies or gold. Pakistan's Federal Shariat Court (FSC) extensively discussed this (14 November 1991 judgment), drawing on Hidaya, Al-Mabsut, Badaa'i al-Sanaa'e, Al Jaziri's Kitabul Fiqh, and scholars like Imam Abu Hanifa, Imam Muhammad, Abu Yousuf, Ghazali, and Ibn Abidin Shami. Citing the Sahih Muslim Hadith on Riba in the exchange of six commodities (including gold/silver as currency), the FSC held that if Rs.100 borrowed becomes Rs.120 after indexation upon repayment a year later, this falls under Riba (both Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl). Al Jaziri's Kitabul Fiqh: a loan/debt requires equality -- a measurable/countable thing lent (e.g. wheat) must be returned in the SAME quantity irrespective of price increase or decrease. Allama Kasani: a loan should not carry any kind of benefit -- e.g. repaying depreciated/stagnant coins with proper coins, or any benefit at repayment time, is illegal, since any stipulated benefit in a loan transaction is Riba (not compensating for anything real). Ibn Qudama similarly holds the borrower must return the SAME as borrowed, regardless of value increase or devaluation, paying the same coins/currency irrespective of currency changes. Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench (Justice Khalilur Rahman) reinforces: Riba/interest cannot be rationalized via indexation, since loans/debts must settle on an equal basis in terms of the loan units (e.g. a Rs.1000 debt is claimable as exactly Rs.1000 by counting -- no more, no less); if this doesn't suit a lender, the Shari'ah-compliant alternative is not a loan at all but rather Bai' Mu'ajjal or a partnership arrangement, where the resource-owner's concerns are addressed via a margin built into the deferred price, or via shared realized profits, not indexation. Al Jaziri's example: A buys 4 pounds of meat on credit at qirsh 5/pound (total 20 qirsh payable); even if meat's price drops to qirsh 2/pound by payment time, A must still pay the full 20 qirsh, not 8 -- because a Qard's repayment obligation is fixed in nominal units, unaffected by subsequent price changes. Lending is a gratuitous/generous act; if the lent value decreases due to inflation, this is effectively an even greater virtue by the lender. The Qur'an encourages giving struggling borrowers extra time. Consequently, the OIC Fiqh Council has categorically forbidden indexing a lent amount to cost-of-living, interest rates, GNP growth rates, gold prices, or other commodities. However, one MAY validly lend in terms of gold or another currency considered less inflation-vulnerable -- but even then, the debt liability itself cannot increase due to subsequent inflation once fixed. A key rationale: when a currency depreciates, its value falls across the board regardless of whether it's lent out or held; indexing a loan to gold specifically to avoid this decline would mean the lender draws a benefit from lending (since the debtor makes good the deficiency while money kept in the lender's own coffer would have simply lost value) -- this benefit-drawing from the loan makes it non-Shari'ah-compliant. The OIC Fiqh Council's 8th session (1993) resolved that creditor and debtor MAY agree, only ON THE DAY OF SETTLEMENT (not before), to settle a debt in a different currency at the exchange rate applicable on that settlement date; this also applies to instalment debts, and parties may at contracting time agree to settle a deferred cost/salary in a specific currency (single payment or instalments) or against a given amount of gold, with actual settlement still following the above same-day rate rule. Critically, a debt contracted in one currency should not be recorded against the debtor in its gold or other-currency counter value, as that would compel him to settle specifically in that alternate form.","examExplanation":"Indexation of loans/debts (to inflation, gold, currency baskets, GNP growth, etc.) is REJECTED by Shari'ah scholars/courts/boards -- treated as Riba (both Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl per Pakistan's FSC). Rule: a loan must be repaid in exactly the SAME nominal units/quantity borrowed, regardless of value change (Al Jaziri, Ibn Qudama, Kasani all agree). Al Jaziri's meat example: even if price drops, the ORIGINAL agreed amount (20 qirsh) is still owed, not a price-adjusted figure. Rationale: indexing to protect against inflation = drawing a benefit from lending = non-Shari'ah-compliant, since the debtor would 'make good' currency depreciation the lender would otherwise also suffer if holding cash himself. Shari'ah alternative to protect against inflation: use Bai' Mu'ajjal or partnership (profit margin/shared risk), not lending with indexation. One MAY lend in gold/inflation-resistant currency from the start, but once fixed, that debt amount cannot then increase further due to inflation. OIC Fiqh Council (1993): currency-of-settlement CAN be changed, but only by mutual agreement made ON THE SETTLEMENT DATE ITSELF (not before), at that date's exchange rate -- pre-agreeing a different currency's fixed counter-value is not allowed.","keyPoints":["Indexing loans/debts to inflation, currency baskets, gold prices, or GNP growth is rejected by Shari'ah scholars, courts, and Islamic bank boards -- treated as Riba (both Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl, per Pakistan's Federal Shariat Court).","The governing rule (Al Jaziri, Ibn Qudama, Kasani): a fungible loan must be repaid in exactly the same nominal quantity/units borrowed, irrespective of subsequent price increase or decrease.","Al Jaziri's meat example illustrates this: even if the market price of the goods later falls, the originally agreed nominal amount remains payable in full.","Rationale against indexation: it would let the lender draw a benefit from lending (protection against inflation the lender would otherwise bear on his own cash too), making the loan non-Shari'ah-compliant.","The Shari'ah-compliant alternative to protect a resource-owner against inflation is not indexed lending, but Bai' Mu'ajjal or a genuine partnership arrangement, where compensation flows through a deferred-price margin or shared realized profits.","One MAY validly lend in gold or another currency perceived as less inflation-vulnerable from the outset -- but once the debt amount is fixed, it still cannot increase further due to subsequent inflation.","The OIC Fiqh Council (8th session, 1993) permits changing the settlement currency of a debt only by mutual agreement made ON THE DAY OF SETTLEMENT itself, at that day's prevailing exchange rate -- not pre-agreed in advance to a fixed alternate-currency value."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["A loan/debt must be repaid in exactly the same nominal quantity/units originally borrowed, regardless of inflation, deflation, or currency devaluation.","Any change of settlement currency for a debt must be agreed by both parties on the day of settlement itself, at the exchange rate prevailing on that date -- not pre-fixed in advance."],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Al Jaziri's meat-purchase example","kind":"textbook","text":"A buys 4 pounds of meat on credit at 5 qirsh/pound (20 qirsh total payable). If the meat's price falls to 2 qirsh/pound by payment time, A must still pay the full 20 qirsh, not 8 -- the originally fixed nominal amount is unaffected by later price changes."},{"title":"Pakistan FSC's Rs.100-to-Rs.120 indexation example","kind":"textbook","text":"If someone borrows Rs.100 from a bank, repayable after one year, and indexation inflates this to Rs.120, this falls into Riba as per the Hadith on exchange of the six commodities -- covering both Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl."}],"commonConfusions":["Students sometimes think indexation is a fair way to protect a lender's real purchasing power; the chapter's rationale explains this actually amounts to drawing a benefit from lending (since currency depreciation affects held cash equally, but indexation makes only the BORROWER absorb that loss on the lender's behalf) -- hence it's non-compliant.","The OIC Fiqh Council's allowance for settling a debt in a different currency is often misread as permitting advance indexation; it strictly requires the currency-change agreement to be made ON THE SETTLEMENT DATE itself, at that date's rate -- not fixed in advance."],"distinctions":["Lending in an inflation-resistant currency/gold from the START (permitted, but amount still fixed once lent) vs indexing an existing loan's repayment amount to inflation/gold AFTER the fact (prohibited)","Same-day currency-of-settlement change by mutual agreement (permitted per OIC Fiqh Council) vs advance indexation to a different currency's fixed counter-value (not permitted)"],"relatedConcepts":["Riba Al-Nasiah","Riba Al-Fadl","Bai' Mu'ajjal","Musharakah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"advanced","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[172,173,174]}}]}],"questions":[{"id":"q7.1","type":"match","prompt":"Match each term to its description.","pairs":[["Qard","Benevolent loan, similar repaid on demand"],["Salaf","Loan for a fixed time (also Salam)"],["Dayn","Liability from a credit transaction"],["‘Ariyah","Free use; same item returned"]],"explanation":"Section 7.2.","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"section":"7.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Define loan-related terms","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.2","type":"tf","prompt":"Lending debased coins on condition that good coins be repaid is permissible because the number of coins is equal.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"An increase in quality is Riba (p. 158).","topicId":"t7.3","chapter":7,"section":"7.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the rule on benefits in loans","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.3","pages":[157,158]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.3","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can Islamic banks not share profit with current account holders under the predominant ruling?","options":["Current accounts are Mudarabah","They are loans not subject to risk, so no return is payable","Profits are too small","AAOIFI forbids current accounts"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 163.","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"section":"7.11","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain current account treatment","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.4","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Murabaha client wants to repay three years early and asks the bank for a contractual rebate. Following AAOIFI, the bank:","options":["Must give a rebate stated in the contract","Cannot commit a rebate in the contract but may give one at its sole discretion","Must charge extra","Must refuse early payment"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 165.","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"section":"7.12","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rebate rules","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,165]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.5","type":"multi","prompt":"Which statements on default penalties are correct according to Chapter 7? (Select all.)","options":["Penalty proceeds go to charity","Compensation can be based on conventional opportunity cost","Penalty clauses cannot be applied to Qard al Hasan","A penalty becomes void if default was beyond the client’s control","Penalties may be credited to the bank’s income"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3],"explanation":"pp. 165–167.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain penalty rules","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.6","type":"application","prompt":"A client deliberately delays paying a Murabaha instalment for three months. Under the Al Baraka Sudan fatwa, the bank may seek compensation calculated as:","options":["A fixed 20% penalty","The bank’s actual realised return on investment during those three months, if any","The prevailing interest rate","Double the instalment"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 166.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply compensation methods","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.7","type":"identify","prompt":"Cheques, drafts and bills of exchange are modern forms of which contract?","options":["Kafalah","Hawalah","Rihn","Ju‘alah"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 168.","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"section":"7.14","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify Hawalah applications","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.8","type":"comparison","prompt":"What distinguishes assignment of debt (Hawalah) from the prohibited sale of debt?","options":["Hawalah is at a discount","In Hawalah there is recourse to the assignor/original debtor if the assignee does not pay","Sale of debt is gratuitous","No difference"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 168.","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"section":"7.14","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Compare Hawalah and debt sale","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.9","type":"scenario","prompt":"A loan agreement states that if the borrower fails to pay, the pledged land automatically becomes the lender’s property in place of the debt. This clause is:","options":["Valid","Invalid — the pledge must remain redeemable; sale via court/power of attorney with excess returned","Required by Shari’ah","Valid only for banks"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 170.","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply pledge rules","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.10","type":"tf","prompt":"The OIC Fiqh Council allows parties, on the day of settlement, to agree to settle a debt in another currency at the exchange rate prevailing on that date.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 174.","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"section":"7.17","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall inflation-related rulings","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[172,174]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.11","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which statement about the Malaysian practice of Bai‘ al Dayn is correct according to the author?","options":["It is accepted by the Jamhoor","It relies on a Shafi‘i view that actually allowed debt sale only at par","The OIC endorsed it","It is permitted at a premium only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 172.","topicId":"t7.16","chapter":7,"section":"7.16","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate debt-sale practice","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.16","pages":[172,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.12","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why gracious extra repayment (Husnal Qadha) cannot be adopted as a system by banks.","keywords":["precondition","system","expected","profit","Riba"],"correctAnswer":"Extra repayment is commendable only when given freely without precondition. As a system, depositors would expect an explicit (customary rate) or implicit (e.g. nominal GNP growth) addition, so the loan would necessarily yield a profit — which is Riba and contrary to Islamic finance.","explanation":"Section 7.11.","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"section":"7.11","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyse gracious repayment","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.13","type":"order","prompt":"Order the steps when a genuinely insolvent debtor cannot pay (Section 7.13.1).","items":["Ensure bankruptcy is not fraudulent","Declare the debtor insolvent (Muflis)","Sell his assets","Distribute proceeds among creditors pro rata; give time for the rest"],"explanation":"p. 167.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe insolvency procedure","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.14","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is the literal meaning of Qard?","options":["To lend","To cut","To trust","To delay"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Property is “cut off” from the lender’s ownership (p. 155).","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"section":"7.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the meaning of Qard","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.15","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does ‘Ariyah differ from Qard?","options":["‘Ariyah carries rent","In ‘Ariyah the same item is returned; in Qard a similar item is returned","Qard is only for animals","There is no difference"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 156.","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"section":"7.2","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish ‘Ariyah and Qard","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.16","type":"identify","prompt":"Before Ghazwa Hunain, from whom did the Prophet (pbuh) borrow 40,000 dirhams for the Islamic State?","options":["Safwan bin Umayyah","Abdullah Ibn Rabi‘ah","Abbas","Ka‘ab bin Malik"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 157.","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"section":"7.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall State borrowing in the Sunnah","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.17","type":"tf","prompt":"According to Malik, once a repayment date is fixed for a Qard, the lender cannot demand earlier payment.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"Other schools treat Qard as callable any time (p. 156 n. 5).","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"section":"7.2","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Compare views on callable loans","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.18","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lender agrees to a loan on condition that the borrower rents his house to the lender. What is the status?","options":["Valid","Illegal — a loan conditioned on another contract (sale, lease or loan) is prohibited","Valid if the rent is fair","Makruh"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 157–158.","topicId":"t7.3","chapter":7,"section":"7.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on conditional loans","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.3","pages":[157,158]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.19","type":"multi","prompt":"Why are conventional deposits and government securities classed as Qard? (Select all.)","options":["The principal is guaranteed","A return is stipulated that is not linked to economic outcome","The bank uses the funds as it wishes and must repay even after loss","Depositors share losses"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"p. 158.","topicId":"t7.4","chapter":7,"section":"7.4","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Classify conventional deposits","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.4","pages":[158,158]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.20","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to verse 2:282, who should dictate the terms of a credit transaction?","options":["The creditor","The party who incurs the liability (or his guardian if unable)","The scribe","A judge"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 159.","topicId":"t7.5","chapter":7,"section":"7.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Qur’anic documentation rules","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.5","pages":[159,159]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.21","type":"tf","prompt":"According to 2:282, spot transactions must always be put in writing.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"There is no blame for not writing spot transactions (p. 159).","topicId":"t7.5","chapter":7,"section":"7.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish spot and credit documentation","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.5","pages":[159,159]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.22","type":"identify","prompt":"Which school allows Qard only in fungibles (things of weight and measure)?","options":["Maliki","Shafi‘i","Hanafi","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":2,"explanation":"p. 159.","topicId":"t7.6","chapter":7,"section":"7.6","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on the subject of loans","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.6","pages":[159,160]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.23","type":"application","prompt":"A merchant lends money in Karachi to be repaid in Dubai because it suits both parties, with no extra cost to the borrower. Is this permissible?","options":["No, it is a benefit from the loan","Yes — like Ibn Zubair’s drafts, a customary indirect benefit costing the borrower nothing is allowed","Only if a fee is paid","Only for gold"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 160.","topicId":"t7.7","chapter":7,"section":"7.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on indirect benefits","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.7","pages":[160,160]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.24","type":"tf","prompt":"Interest on a loan from a State to its citizens is exempt from the prohibition of Riba.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"No exemption for any relationship (p. 160).","topicId":"t7.7","chapter":7,"section":"7.7","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the universality of the prohibition","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.7","pages":[160,160]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.25","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why can government bonds not be “sold” like goods to realise time value?","options":["They are illiquid","They represent money/value only and cannot serve as Mabi‘","They are always interest-free","They are foreign"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 161.","topicId":"t7.8","chapter":7,"section":"7.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why money-like instruments cannot be traded","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.8","pages":[160,161]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.26","type":"multi","prompt":"Which duties of the debtor are described in Section 7.9? (Select all.)","options":["Repay in fulfilment of the promise","Have a firm intention to repay when borrowing","Thank the creditor on repayment","Pay a fixed return"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2],"explanation":"pp. 161–162.","topicId":"t7.9","chapter":7,"section":"7.9","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall duties of the debtor","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.9","pages":[161,162]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.27","type":"scenario","prompt":"A debtor is genuinely destitute. Does he have a right to more time?","options":["Yes, as his legal right","No — creditors are encouraged to give time, but a destitute debtor cannot claim it as a right; earnings above basic food go to repayment","The debt is cancelled","The State pays automatically"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 162.","topicId":"t7.10","chapter":7,"section":"7.10","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply creditor–debtor instructions","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.10","pages":[162,162]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.28","type":"application","prompt":"An Islamic bank wants to pay a fixed “gift” every year to current account holders. What does Chapter 7 say?","options":["Allowed as Husnal Qadha","Not allowed as a system — it would make a loan yield a return","Allowed if under 2%","Required by AAOIFI"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 163.","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"section":"7.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on gifts to current accounts","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.29","type":"identify","prompt":"In which case did the Prophet (pbuh) say “You got yourself and the other party involved in Riba”?","options":["Banu Nadhir","Miqdad remitting 10 of 100 dirhams for early payment","Ka‘ab and Ibn Abi Hadrad","Jabir’s debt"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 164.","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"section":"7.12","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall traditions on remission","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,165]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.30","type":"comparison","prompt":"How do jurists reconcile the traditions on remitting part of a debt?","options":["All remission is Riba","Remission is allowed for due debts (Duyun Halah) but not as a discount on debts not yet due (Duyun Mu’ajjalah)","All remission is allowed","Only for Murabaha"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 164.","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"section":"7.12","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Reconcile remission traditions","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,165]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.31","type":"tf","prompt":"Some later Hanafi jurists held that in Murabaha–Mu’ajjal the creditor must remit the profit for the remaining period if the debtor pays early.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"But the majority of contemporary scholars and AAOIFI do not allow contractual rebates (p. 165).","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"section":"7.12","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall views on prepayment rebate","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,165]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.32","type":"mcq","prompt":"How should compensation to a bank for a client’s default be determined, according to Section 7.13?","options":["At the conventional interest rate","By a court or independent committee based on actual loss, not opportunity cost","By the bank alone","At a fixed 10%"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 166.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the basis of compensation","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.33","type":"scenario","prompt":"A client defaults on Qard al Hasan. Can the bank impose a late-payment penalty?","options":["Yes, paid to charity","No — penalties are not allowed on Qard al Hasan; the creditor should give more time","Yes, as income","Only after one year"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Penalties apply to Dayn from sale/exchange, not to pure loans (pp. 166–167).","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Distinguish Qard and Dayn for penalties","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.34","type":"multi","prompt":"When does a penalty provision become void, per the OIC Fiqh Council? (Select all.)","options":["The client proves the failure was beyond his control","The client proves the bank suffered no loss","The client is rich","The delay is short"],"correctAnswer":[0,1],"explanation":"p. 167.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall when penalties lapse","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.35","type":"identify","prompt":"According to the majority, who has the first right to an item sold on credit that is still intact with an insolvent buyer?","options":["All creditors pro rata","The seller who finds his same goods","The State","The buyer’s heirs"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Abu Hanifa favours distribution among all creditors (p. 167).","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall insolvency rules","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.36","type":"match","prompt":"Match the Hawalah term to its meaning.","pairs":[["Restricted Hawalah","Paid from the assignor’s property held by the payer"],["Unrestricted Hawalah","Payer uses own funds, with recourse to the assignor"],["Suftajah","Document for payment elsewhere through a second party"]],"explanation":"pp. 167–168.","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"section":"7.14","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Hawalah terminology","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.37","type":"tf","prompt":"An assignee in Hawalah may charge a fee for accepting the debt, since he provides a service.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Hawalah is non-commutative; no remuneration for the assignment itself (p. 168).","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"section":"7.14","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Apply the gratuitous nature of Hawalah","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.38","type":"scenario","prompt":"Several people become sureties for one debt one after another. How is liability shared?","options":["Each is liable for his share only","Each is liable for the whole debt","Only the first surety is liable","None are liable"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Joint sureties at the same time share; successive sureties are each liable in full (p. 169).","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply surety liability rules","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.39","type":"mcq","prompt":"If a pledged car is destroyed without the bank’s negligence, who bears the loss?","options":["The bank (pledgee)","The pledger/debtor, who still owes the debt","Shared equally","The insurer only"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"“For him is its Ghunm and upon him its Ghurm” (p. 170).","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply risk in pledge","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.40","type":"application","prompt":"A bank holds a pledged house and leases it out for more than its maintenance cost and customary service charge. Who gets the excess rent?","options":["The bank","The pledger","Charity","The tenant"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 172.","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply rules on benefit from pledge","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.41","type":"identify","prompt":"Which right lets an Islamic bank retain another’s property until his debt is paid?","options":["Possessory lien","Shuf‘ah","Hawalah","Khiyar"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"By analogy with a cash seller retaining goods until paid (p. 172).","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the right of lien","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q7.42","type":"tf","prompt":"The OIC Islamic Fiqh Council, including Malaysia’s representatives, unanimously approved the prohibition of Bai‘ al Dayn.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 172.","topicId":"t7.16","chapter":7,"section":"7.16","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the OIC ruling on sale of debt","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.16","pages":[172,172]},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["q-ch7-24"],"explanationAlt":"True. The chapter states the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council, which has representation of all Islamic countries including Malaysia, approved the prohibition of Bai' al-Dayn unanimously, without a single dissent."},{"id":"q7.43","type":"short","prompt":"Explain why lending by indexing to gold to protect against inflation involves Riba.","keywords":["depreciation","benefit","gold","Riba"],"correctAnswer":"Depreciation affects the currency equally whether the lender keeps it or lends it. If the lender indexes the loan to gold, the borrower makes good the loss of value while money kept by the lender would have lost value, so the lender draws a benefit from the loan — and a loan that draws benefit is Riba. One may lend in gold itself, but the debt then cannot increase.","explanation":"pp. 173–174.","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"section":"7.17","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain why indexation is Riba","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[172,174]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch7-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which term denotes a debt/liability arising from ANY credit transaction (e.g. sale, purchase, or rent), broader in scope than Qard?","explanation":"Dayn is defined as a debt incurred by way of rent, sale, purchase, or any other transaction that leaves it as a debt -- broader than Qard, which specifically refers to a loan given as help/charity for a certain time or on demand.","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"section":"7.2","topicLabel":"The Terms Defined","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah, and explain why only the principal is repayable on a loan.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,156]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Salaf","Dayn","'Ariyah","Hibah"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-2","type":"comparison","prompt":"What is the key structural difference between Qard and 'Ariyah?","explanation":"In 'Ariyah, exactly the borrowed commodity has to be returned; in Qard, the borrower pays back a similar amount of the loaned commodity, since full ownership of the loaned item transfers to him.","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"section":"7.2","topicLabel":"The Terms Defined","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah, and explain why only the principal is repayable on a loan.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[156]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Qard requires returning the exact same item; 'Ariyah allows returning a similar amount","'Ariyah requires returning the exact same item borrowed; Qard requires only a similar amount/item since ownership fully transfers","There is no real difference between the two","Qard applies only to money; 'Ariyah applies only to animals"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-3","type":"scenario","prompt":"A lends B 100 debased (lower-quality) silver coins, on the condition that B repays 100 sound (higher-quality) silver coins. Is this permissible?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly uses this exact example (via Ibn Qudama): lending debased coins on condition of repayment in sound coins is illegal, because the increase, whether in quantity or in quality, constitutes Riba per unanimous jurist agreement.","topicId":"t7.3","chapter":7,"section":"7.3","topicLabel":"Illegality of Commercial Interest","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain why commercial interest is illegal under Shari'ah and how Islamic banks' deposit and financing structures relate to Qard and Dayn.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.3","pages":[157,158]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since the number of coins repaid is the same","No -- any loan condition creating a benefit above the principal, whether in quantity OR quality, is void and constitutes Riba","Yes, as long as B agrees willingly","No, but only because gold and silver are treated differently from other metals"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-4","type":"short","prompt":"Why are current accounts at conventional (and Islamic) banks categorized as Qard rather than as investment/partnership deposits?","explanation":"Current accounts are treated as loans because banks are as much liable to current account holders as to fixed account holders, with the principal fully guaranteed regardless of business outcome.","topicId":"t7.4","chapter":7,"section":"7.4","topicLabel":"Loaning and the Banking System","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why commercial interest is illegal under Shari'ah and how Islamic banks' deposit and financing structures relate to Qard and Dayn.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.4","pages":[158]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because the bank is fully liable to repay the current account holder in full on demand, regardless of the bank's profit or loss -- the principal is guaranteed and there is no risk-sharing, which matches the definition of Qard (a loan), not a profit/loss-sharing investment."},{"id":"q-ch7-5","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Per Qur'an 2:282-283, spot (on-the-spot) transactions require the same written documentation as credit/future-payment transactions.","explanation":"False. The verse's documentation and witness requirements apply specifically to future-obligation/credit transactions; for on-the-spot transactions, no written evidence is required, though witnesses are still recommended.","topicId":"t7.5","chapter":7,"section":"7.5","topicLabel":"Qur'anic Guidance on Loans and Debts","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah, and explain why only the principal is repayable on a loan.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.5","pages":[159]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch7-6","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which school restricts Qard to fungibles only (similars by weight/measure), against the other three major schools which permit any saleable item except human beings?","explanation":"According to the Hanafi school, a loan should consist of fungibles only; the other three schools (Maliki, Shafi'e, Hanbali) allow Qard of any saleable commodity except human beings, as the Sunnah shows the Prophet took Qard of animals (nonfungibles).","topicId":"t7.6","chapter":7,"section":"7.6","topicLabel":"The Substance of Loans","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Distinguish Qard, Salaf, Dayn and 'Ariyah, and explain why only the principal is repayable on a loan.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.6","pages":[159,160]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Maliki","Shafi'e","Hanafi","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch7-7","type":"scenario","prompt":"An investor argues: 'Since a credit sale price can legitimately exceed the cash price in trading, I should be able to charge extra for the delay when someone repays a loan I gave them.' Is this reasoning valid per the chapter?","explanation":"The chapter draws a sharp line: while a difference between cash and credit price is approved in trade/business, no value can be assigned to the time given for payment of a Qard/Dayn receivable once its amount is mutually stipulated -- these are virtuous acts, not commercial exchanges.","topicId":"t7.8","chapter":7,"section":"7.8","topicLabel":"Time Value of Money in Loans and Debts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah treatment of time value of money in loans/debts versus in trade.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.8","pages":[160,161]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since both cases involve a delay in payment","No -- time value of money is approved in genuine trade/business, but NOT in Qard or Dayn, which are virtuous acts from which the lender cannot take any benefit","Yes, but only up to a maximum of 5% of the loan value","No, because trade profit margins are also illegal"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-8","type":"short","prompt":"According to the chapter, what did the Prophet equate wilful default or procrastination in debt payment to?","explanation":"The chapter states that wilful default or procrastination in payment of due debt has been equated by the holy Prophet to injustice.","topicId":"t7.9","chapter":7,"section":"7.9","topicLabel":"Instructions for the Debtor","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the Shari'ah instructions given to debtors and creditors, including Husnal Qadha and the rules on remitting part of a debt or granting a prepayment rebate.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.9","pages":[161]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"identify","correctAnswer":"Injustice (Zulm)"},{"id":"q-ch7-9","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: A creditor can be legally compelled to give a debtor more time to repay, even if the creditor does not wish to.","explanation":"False. The chapter states that if the creditor is not inclined to give more time, he cannot be compelled to do so, and the debtor would then be required to pay one way or another -- even a destitute debtor is not automatically entitled to more time.","topicId":"t7.10","chapter":7,"section":"7.10","topicLabel":"Instructions for the Creditor","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Shari'ah instructions given to debtors and creditors, including Husnal Qadha and the rules on remitting part of a debt or granting a prepayment rebate.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.10","pages":[162]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch7-10","type":"scenario","prompt":"An Islamic bank considers adopting a standard policy of always paying depositors an amount above principal on their Qard-based accounts, as a matter of routine practice. Is this consistent with Husnal Qadha?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states gracious repayment cannot be adopted as a system, because this would mean a loan would necessarily yield a profit, which contradicts the discretionary, virtuous nature of Husnal Qadha.","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"section":"7.11","topicLabel":"Husnal Qadha","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"State the Shari'ah instructions given to debtors and creditors, including Husnal Qadha and the rules on remitting part of a debt or granting a prepayment rebate.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, since Husnal Qadha explicitly encourages this","No -- Husnal Qadha must remain a matter of individual discretion; systematizing it would mean a loan necessarily yields profit, which is not permitted","Yes, as long as the extra amount is small","No, because Husnal Qadha applies only to individual, not institutional, lenders"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-11","type":"comparison","prompt":"A debt is not yet due -- its payment date has been mutually settled for a future time. The creditor offers to accept a smaller amount now, in exchange for early payment. What is the ruling?","explanation":"The chapter distinguishes Duyun Halah (already-due debts, where remission for early payment IS allowed) from Duyun Mu'ajjalah (not-yet-due debts, where remission is Riba) -- this scenario describes the latter, matching the condemned Miqdad bin Aswad case.","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"section":"7.12","topicLabel":"Remitting a Part of a Loan and Prepayment Rebate","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"State the Shari'ah instructions given to debtors and creditors, including Husnal Qadha and the rules on remitting part of a debt or granting a prepayment rebate.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,164]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Permitted, following the Bani al-Nadhir tradition","Not permitted -- remission for early payment of a NOT-YET-DUE debt (Duyun Mu'ajjalah) is treated as Riba, per Imam Malik and the Miqdad bin Aswad precedent","Permitted, since both parties freely agree to it","Permitted only if the discount is under 10%"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-12","type":"mcq","prompt":"What is AAOIFI's position on prepayment rebates in Murabaha?","explanation":"AAOIFI Shari'ah Standards prohibit a contractually committed rebate (since Murabaha price must be fixed once and for all), but allow the bank to give a rebate at its sole discretion for early payment, absent any prior commitment.","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"section":"7.12","topicLabel":"Remitting a Part of a Loan and Prepayment Rebate","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Shari'ah instructions given to debtors and creditors, including Husnal Qadha and the rules on remitting part of a debt or granting a prepayment rebate.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[165]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Rebates are entirely prohibited under all circumstances","A contractual rebate commitment is prohibited (the price must be fixed once and for all), but a discretionary rebate given solely at the bank's own choice is permitted","Rebates are mandatory for all early-paying clients","Rebates are permitted only for government clients"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-13","type":"scenario","prompt":"A client defaults on a pure Qard al-Hasan loan from an Islamic bank. Can the bank impose a financial penalty on this default?","explanation":"The chapter is explicit: the OIC Fiqh Council differentiates pure loan contracts (Qard, especially Qard al-Hasan) from debt contracts (Dayn); penalty clauses are permitted in the latter but not in the former, since imposing a penalty in a loan contract is usury in the strict sense.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","topicLabel":"Penalty on Default","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah-compliant approach to penalties on default, liquidated damages, and insolvency (Muflis).","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[166,167]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, penalty clauses are permitted for all types of financial obligations","No -- penalty/compensation for default is permitted for Dayn (sale/exchange-created debt) but NOT for Qard (pure loan); imposing a penalty on a pure loan default is usury in the strict sense","Yes, but only up to the amount of the original loan","No, because Qard al-Hasan loans cannot legally default"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-14","type":"short","prompt":"Where must penalty proceeds collected from a wilfully defaulting Dayn client be directed, and why?","explanation":"This rule is central to how Shari'ah bodies (OIC Fiqh Council, AAOIFI, Pakistan's Shariat Appellate Bench) reconcile allowing penalty clauses with the prohibition of Riba.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","topicLabel":"Penalty on Default","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah-compliant approach to penalties on default, liquidated damages, and insolvency (Muflis).","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[166]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"To charity, not to the creditor's own income -- penalties on default cannot become a source of income for the creditor, which keeps the penalty conceptually distinct from interest or Murabaha profit."},{"id":"q-ch7-15","type":"mcq","prompt":"What kind of 'loss' calculation is explicitly excluded when a bank claims liquidated damages/compensation from a defaulting client?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly states 'actual loss' should not be the loss in terms of conventional 'opportunity cost' -- it must be proven to the court's/arbitrator's satisfaction as an actual, realized loss.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13","topicLabel":"Penalty on Default","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah-compliant approach to penalties on default, liquidated damages, and insolvency (Muflis).","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[166]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Actual, proven loss based on realized investment returns","Conventional 'opportunity cost'","Charity-directed penalty amounts","Losses proven to a court or arbitrator"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-16","type":"scenario","prompt":"A seller sold goods on credit to a buyer who has now become genuinely insolvent (Muflis). The exact same goods are still with the buyer, unchanged. Who has priority to reclaim them, per the majority view?","explanation":"The chapter cites the Hadith ('if a man finds his same things with a bankrupt, he has more right to take them back than anyone else') and states the majority of jurists hold the original seller has first right, while Imam Abu Hanifa instead favors pro rata distribution among all creditors.","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"section":"7.13.1","topicLabel":"Insolvency of the Debtor","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain the Shari'ah-compliant approach to penalties on default, liquidated damages, and insolvency (Muflis).","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13.1","pages":[167]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["All creditors equally, on a pro rata basis, per Imam Abu Hanifa's view (majority position)","The original seller has first right to reclaim the goods, per the Hadith and majority of jurists","The court automatically liquidates and keeps the proceeds","No one may reclaim goods from an insolvent debtor"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-17","type":"short","prompt":"Define Hawalah and explain what makes it permissible where a plain sale of debt is not.","explanation":"This recourse feature is the critical distinguishing element the chapter emphasizes repeatedly between prohibited debt sale and permissible debt assignment.","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"section":"7.14","topicLabel":"Hawalah (Assignment of Debt)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"Hawalah is an agreement by which a debtor is freed from a debt by another party becoming responsible for it (assignment of debt). It is permissible, unlike a plain sale of debt, because Hawalah preserves recourse to the original debtor (assignor) if the new debtor (assignee) fails to pay -- a plain sale of debt has no such recourse, introducing Gharar and Riba."},{"id":"q-ch7-18","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Like Kafalah and Ijarah, Hawalah's own effectiveness can be validly delayed to a future date.","explanation":"False. Hawalah must take effect immediately -- it cannot be suspended, concluded temporarily, or made contingent on an unlikely future event, though the payment DATE of the transferred debt itself can be deferred to a mutually specified date.","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"section":"7.14","topicLabel":"Hawalah (Assignment of Debt)","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[168]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch7-19","type":"scenario","prompt":"Three people, one after another (not simultaneously), each separately become surety for the same $10,000 debt. How much is each surety liable for?","explanation":"The chapter distinguishes joint/simultaneous sureties (each liable only for their own share) from sureties who become surety one after another (successively) -- in the latter case, each one is liable for the WHOLE debt.","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15","topicLabel":"Kafalah and Rihn","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[169]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Each is liable for one-third ($3,333)","Each successive surety is liable for the WHOLE debt ($10,000)","Only the first surety is liable; the others are released","Only the last surety is liable"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-20","type":"scenario","prompt":"A pledged car is destroyed in a fire, with no proven negligence on the part of the bank (pledgee) holding it. Who bears this loss?","explanation":"Per the Hadith cited ('it is from the pledger and for him is its Ghunm and upon him is its Ghurm'), the pledger remains the owner and bears the pledge's risk and reward; the pledgee, as trustee, is not liable for loss absent proven negligence, and can still recover the loan from the pledger.","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15.1","topicLabel":"Risk and Reward in Pledge","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15.1","pages":[170]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The bank (pledgee), since it had possession","The pledger (debtor), since he remains the owner of the pledge's risk and reward (Ghunm/Ghurm); the bank, as trustee, is not liable absent proven negligence","The loss is split equally between both parties","Neither party bears the loss; the debt is automatically cancelled"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-21","type":"short","prompt":"What does the Hadith phrase 'pledge cannot be foreclosed' actually mean, per the chapter's clarification?","explanation":"The chapter explicitly clarifies this phrase does not bar court-sanctioned recovery -- it bars the pledgee from simply seizing/appropriating the pledged item outright.","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15.1","topicLabel":"Risk and Reward in Pledge","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15.1","pages":[171]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"It does not mean the pledgee can never sell the pledge to recover the debt -- rather, it means the pledge should remain redeemable and not be wrongfully appropriated by the pledgee (as was pre-Islamic practice); on default, recovery must go through a court-sanctioned sale process, not simple seizure."},{"id":"q-ch7-22","type":"comparison","prompt":"Under which school's view is a pledgee's benefit from a pledge conditioned on (i) the underlying debt arising from a sale (not Qard), (ii) the benefit being stipulated at contract time, and (iii) a specified time period?","explanation":"The Maliki school specifically conditions pledgee benefit on all three factors: the loan not being of the Qard type but from a sale transaction, the benefit being stipulated at contract time, and its period being specified.","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15.2","topicLabel":"Benefits from Pledge","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15.2","pages":[171]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hanafi (majority view)","Shafi'e","Maliki","Hanbali"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch7-23","type":"scenario","prompt":"A Malaysian Islamic bank cites the Shafi'e school to justify trading a debt-based Sukuk at a discount in the secondary market. What does the chapter say about this citation?","explanation":"The chapter (citing Rosly and Sanusi) clarifies that Malaysian experts citing the Shafi'e school for discounted Bai' al-Dayn misapply it -- Shafi'e jurists allowed debt sale only at par value, not at a discount or premium.","topicId":"t7.16","chapter":7,"section":"7.16","topicLabel":"Bai' al-Dayn","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain why Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) is prohibited and why indexing loans/debts to inflation is not permitted.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.16","pages":[172]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The citation is accurate; Shafi'e jurists permit discounted debt sale generally","The citation is a misapplication -- Shafi'e jurists allowed debt sale only AT its par (face) value, never at a discount or premium","The citation is accurate only for gold-backed Sukuk","The Shafi'e school does not address debt sale at all"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-25","type":"scenario","prompt":"A buys 4 pounds of meat on credit at 5 qirsh per pound (20 qirsh total payable). By the time of payment, the market price of meat has fallen to 2 qirsh per pound. How much must A pay, per the chapter's example?","explanation":"This is the exact example given (via Al Jaziri) in the chapter: A must pay the full originally-agreed 20 qirsh, not an amount adjusted for the later price change -- a loan/debt must be repaid in the same nominal quantity/units originally agreed.","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"section":"7.17","topicLabel":"Impact of Inflation on Loans/Debts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain why Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) is prohibited and why indexing loans/debts to inflation is not permitted.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[174]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["8 qirsh, reflecting the new market price","20 qirsh -- the originally agreed nominal amount, unaffected by the later price change","14 qirsh, split the difference","Nothing, since the meat is now worth less than expected"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-26","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the chapter argue that indexing an existing loan's repayment amount to inflation (e.g. via gold) would make the loan non-Shari'ah-compliant?","explanation":"The chapter's key rationale: when currency depreciates, its value falls whether held or lent; indexing a loan to gold to protect against this would mean the lender draws a benefit specifically from lending (which cash kept in his own coffer would not provide), making it non-compliant.","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"section":"7.17","topicLabel":"Impact of Inflation on Loans/Debts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) is prohibited and why indexing loans/debts to inflation is not permitted.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[173,174]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because gold is never a valid measure of value in Islamic law","Because it would let the lender draw a benefit from lending -- the debtor makes good the currency's depreciation on the lender's behalf, a benefit the lender wouldn't get from money kept in his own coffer","Because indexation is technically impossible to calculate","Because only governments are allowed to index currency"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-27","type":"scenario","prompt":"Per the OIC Fiqh Council's 1993 resolution, can a creditor and debtor agree, at the time of ORIGINALLY contracting a debt, to peg its future settlement to a fixed amount of gold?","explanation":"The OIC Fiqh Council resolution explicitly allows currency-of-settlement changes only by agreement made on the day of settlement (not before), at the prevailing rate that day; a debt should not be recorded against the debtor in a fixed gold/currency counter-value in advance.","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"section":"7.17","topicLabel":"Impact of Inflation on Loans/Debts","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain why Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) is prohibited and why indexing loans/debts to inflation is not permitted.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[174]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Yes, this is exactly what the resolution permits","No -- currency-of-settlement changes may only be agreed ON THE DAY OF SETTLEMENT itself, at that day's exchange rate; a debt cannot be pre-recorded against a fixed gold/other-currency counter-value","Yes, but only for debts under a specified amount","No, currency-of-settlement can never be changed under any circumstances"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-28","type":"short","prompt":"What Shari'ah-compliant alternative does the chapter recommend for a resource-owner who wants protection against inflation, instead of simply lending money?","explanation":"This is Justice Khalilur Rahman's point cited in the chapter: instead of Riba-tainted indexed lending, the Shari'ah-compliant path is trade (Bai' Mu'ajjal) or partnership, where compensation flows through legitimate, risk-based mechanisms.","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"section":"7.17","topicLabel":"Impact of Inflation on Loans/Debts","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Explain why Bai' al-Dayn (sale of debt) is prohibited and why indexing loans/debts to inflation is not permitted.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[173,174]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Use Bai' Mu'ajjal (a deferred-payment sale, where a margin can legitimately be built into the price) or a genuine partnership (Musharakah-type) arrangement, where the resource-owner's concerns are addressed through the deferred-price margin or through shared, realized profits -- rather than lending with indexation."},{"id":"q-ch7-29","type":"mcq","prompt":"What should a debtor genuinely unable to pay his debt do, according to the Shari'ah instructions in the chapter?","explanation":"The chapter instructs that in desperate circumstances when the debtor is really unable to pay, he should take the creditor into his confidence and regret his inability to pay the debt, rather than defaulting silently.","topicId":"t7.9","chapter":7,"section":"7.9","topicLabel":"Instructions for the Debtor","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the Shari'ah instructions given to debtors and creditors, including Husnal Qadha and the rules on remitting part of a debt or granting a prepayment rebate.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.9","pages":[161]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Simply default silently and hope the creditor doesn't notice","Take the creditor into his confidence and express regret at his inability to pay","Declare bankruptcy immediately without informing the creditor","Request the government to cancel the debt automatically"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch7-30","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are listed in the chapter as types of guarantee a bank can call for to secure its loans?","explanation":"The chapter lists letters of guarantee, use of post-dated cheques, promissory notes, freezing cash deposits, third-party guarantees, Hamish Jiddiyah, and 'Arbun as guarantee types a bank can call for. Unsecured verbal promises alone are not listed as a guarantee mechanism.","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"section":"7.15","topicLabel":"Kafalah and Rihn","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe Hawalah (assignment of debt) and Kafalah/Rihn (guarantee/pledge), and how each differs from a prohibited sale of debt.","source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[169,170]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Letters of guarantee","Hamish Jiddiyah","'Arbun","Post-dated cheques","Unsecured verbal promises alone"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3]}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f7.1","front":"Qard","back":"Giving anything of value to another by way of virtue, to be repaid in same or similar amount on demand or at the settled time.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.2","front":"Qard vs ‘Ariyah","back":"Qard: a similar item is returned. ‘Ariyah: free use of a nonconsumable item; exactly the same item is returned.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.3","front":"Is a benefit in quality on a loan Riba?","back":"Yes — e.g. lending debased coins for good coins; any stipulated benefit in quantity or quality, gift or ancillary contract is Riba.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t7.3","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.3","pages":[157,158]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.4","front":"Current accounts in Islamic banks","back":"Treated as loans: guaranteed, repayable on call, no profit weightage; service charges allowed; OIC: repayment unaffected by bank solvency.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.5","front":"Husnal Qadha","back":"Gracious repayment — paying more than principal without precondition; commendable individually but cannot be adopted as a system.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.6","front":"Remission for early payment: due vs not-yet-due debt","back":"Allowed for Duyun Halah (due debts) by almost all; not for Duyun Mu’ajjalah (not yet due).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,165]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.7","front":"AAOIFI on prepayment rebate in Murabaha","back":"Contractual rebate prohibited (price fixed once); a discretionary rebate not committed in the contract is allowed.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,165]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.8","front":"Penalty vs compensation on default","back":"Penalty clause: proceeds to charity. Compensation: actual loss (not opportunity cost), decided by court/arbitrator; none on Qard al Hasan.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.9","front":"When does a penalty clause become void (OIC)?","back":"When the client proves failure was beyond his control, or that the bank incurred no loss.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.10","front":"Hawalah","back":"Assignment of debt: a debtor is freed by another becoming responsible. Gratuitous, at face value, immediate, binding, with recourse.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.11","front":"Banking products that are forms of Hawalah","back":"Cheques, drafts, pay orders, remittances, promissory notes, bills of exchange, ODs, endorsements.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.12","front":"Kafalah vs Rihn","back":"Kafalah: third-party surety for the debt. Rihn: the debtor pledges an asset, held by creditor as trust.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.13","front":"Who bears the risk of a pledged asset?","back":"The pledger (owner): “its Ghunm is for him and its Ghurm upon him.” Pledgee is a trustee unless negligent.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.14","front":"Can a pledgee bank use a pledged house?","back":"It may benefit in return for maintenance, charge a customary service rate or lease it; income above expenses/customary rate goes to the pledger.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.15","front":"OIC position on Bai‘ al Dayn","back":"Prohibited unanimously (including Malaysia); Shafi‘i jurists allowed debt sale only at par.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.16","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.16","pages":[172,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.16","front":"FSC Pakistan (1991) on indexation","back":"Indexing Rs.100 to Rs.120 falls under Riba Al-Nasiah and Riba Al-Fadl — not permitted.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[172,174]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.17","front":"Salaf vs Qard","back":"Salaf: a loan for a fixed time (also another name for Salam). Qard: payable on demand.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155,157]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.18","front":"Benefit in quality from a loan","back":"Lending debased coins for good ones, or “better units” later, is Riba — quantity or quality makes no difference.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t7.3","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.3","pages":[157,158]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.19","front":"Documentation in 2:282","back":"Credit deals: write them down, liable party dictates, two witnesses. Spot deals: no blame for not writing. On a journey: pledge with possession.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.5","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.5","pages":[159,159]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.20","front":"Customary indirect benefits in loans","back":"Allowed if they cost the borrower nothing — e.g. repayment in another city (Ibn Zubair’s drafts).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.7","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.7","pages":[160,160]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.21","front":"Can currency notes be leased?","back":"No — notes and government bonds are money, not Mabi‘; they cannot be sold or leased to earn time value.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t7.8","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.8","pages":[160,161]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.22","front":"Abu Hanifa on defaulting debtors","back":"Imprison for two to three months, then inquire; release if genuinely unable. Malikis/Shafi‘is: do not arrest a troubled debtor.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.10","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.10","pages":[162,162]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.23","front":"Al Baraka Sudan measure of compensation","back":"Bank’s actual realised return during the delay; nothing if it earned nothing; debtor must be solvent and deliberately late.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.24","front":"Muflis","back":"An insolvent/bankrupt debtor: assets sold and proceeds shared pro rata; remaining debt gets time.","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.25","front":"What happens if a Hawalah assignee dies bankrupt?","back":"Majority: the obligation returns to the assignor — no debt goes unpaid.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.26","front":"Can a pledge be foreclosed?","back":"No automatic takeover of the pledge for the debt; the pledgee may have it sold (court or irrevocable power of attorney) and return any excess.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f7.27","front":"OIC 1993 on settling debts in another currency","back":"Allowed if agreed on the settlement day at that day’s rate — not recorded in advance against the debtor in gold or another currency.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[172,174]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-1","front":"Define Qard.","back":"To give anything of value into another's ownership by way of virtue, so the recipient may benefit from it, on condition that the same or a similar amount is paid back on demand or at a settled time.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[155]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-2","front":"Distinguish Qard from 'Ariyah.","back":"In Qard, ownership of the loaned item fully transfers and a SIMILAR amount is repaid. In 'Ariyah, the EXACT SAME item (not a substitute) must be returned -- it's a gratuitous loan of usufruct of a nonconsumable item.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[156]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-3","front":"Distinguish Salaf from Qard from Dayn.","back":"Qard = loan payable on demand (a specific kind of Salaf). Salaf = loan for a fixed term (also = another name for Salam). Dayn = any debt arising from a credit transaction (broader than Qard) -- e.g. from sale, purchase, or rent.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.2","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.2","pages":[156,157]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-4","front":"What is the unanimous jurist rule on any loan condition creating a benefit above the principal?","back":"It is void, regardless of whether the increase is in quantity or in quality (Ibn Qudama's Al-Mughni, citing unanimous agreement). E.g. lending debased coins for repayment in sound coins is illegal, even though the 'increase' is only in quality.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.3","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.3","pages":[157,158]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-5","front":"Why are conventional bank deposits and current accounts classified as Qard?","back":"Because the principal is guaranteed and a fixed or activity-independent return is stipulated (for deposits); banks bear full liability for repayment even on loss -- current accounts are equally the bank's liability as fixed accounts.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t7.4","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.4","pages":[158]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-6","front":"What does Qur'an 2:282-283 instruct regarding credit transactions?","back":"To document them via a faithful scribe, have the liable party (or guardian) dictate terms without diminishing what's owed, and secure two witnesses (2 men, or 1 man + 2 women). Spot transactions don't require writing. If no scribe (e.g. traveling), a pledge with possession (Rihn Maqbudah) may serve instead.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.5","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.5","pages":[159]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-7","front":"How does the Hanafi view on what can be loaned (Qard) differ from the other three major schools?","back":"Hanafi: Qard restricted to fungibles only (similars by weight/measure). Maliki, Shafi'e, Hanbali: Qard allowed for any saleable commodity except human beings (e.g. animals), provided the amount/value is known without doubt.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.6","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.6","pages":[159,160]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-8","front":"Under what circumstance may a creditor accept a gift from a borrower after making a loan?","back":"Only if such gift-exchange was already customary between them BEFORE the loan was advanced -- otherwise, the Prophet said the creditor must refrain from accepting a post-loan gift.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.7","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.7","pages":[160]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-9","front":"Where does Islamic law recognize time value of money, and where does it not?","back":"Recognized in TRADE (a credit price may legitimately exceed a cash price). NOT recognized in Qard or Dayn -- once a loan/debt amount is fixed, no value can be added for the delay in payment; $100 must be repaid as exactly $100.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.8","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.8","pages":[160,161]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-10","front":"What warning did the Prophet give about wilful default and unpaid debt after death?","back":"Wilful default/procrastination in payment is equated to injustice; leaving unpaid debt after death (with no one to pay it) is described as the greatest sin after the Kaba'ir. A debtor able to pay but refusing can be arrested and embarrassed.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.9","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.9","pages":[161]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-11","front":"Compare the majority (Maliki/Shafi'e) and Hanafi views on punishing a genuinely struggling debtor.","back":"Majority (Maliki, Shafi'e): no punishment/arrest for genuinely struggling debtors -- give more time instead. Abu Hanifa: imprison 2-3 months, then reassess his capability; acquit if genuinely unable to pay.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.10","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.10","pages":[162]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-12","front":"Define Husnal Qadha, and state its key limitation.","back":"Gracious payment of a loan/debt in excess of the principal, without a precondition -- commendable and Sunnah-consistent. Key limitation: it must remain a matter of INDIVIDUAL DISCRETION and can never be systematized, since that would make loans structurally profit-yielding.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[162,163]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-13","front":"Are current account deposits in Islamic banks eligible for a profit share?","back":"No -- they are treated as Qard (fully repayable on demand regardless of bank solvency), bear no risk, and so are not eligible for a profit share. Some boards allow nonregular, uninduced gifts/prizes, though most disfavor this.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[163]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-14","front":"Distinguish Duyun Halah from Duyun Mu'ajjalah regarding remission for early payment.","back":"Duyun Halah (already-due debt): remission for early payment of the rest IS allowed by nearly all jurists (not as a precondition). Duyun Mu'ajjalah (settled future due date, not yet due): remission for early payment is NOT allowed -- it's Riba (Imam Malik's clear position).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[164]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-15","front":"What was the Prophet's ruling in the Miqdad bin Aswad case (90 dirhams accepted instead of a 100-dirham not-yet-due debt)?","back":"The Prophet condemned it: 'You got yourself and the other party involved in Riba.' This illustrates that remitting part of a NOT-YET-DUE debt for early payment is Riba.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[164]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-16","front":"What is AAOIFI's position on prepayment rebates in Murabaha?","back":"A CONTRACTUAL rebate commitment is prohibited (Murabaha price must be fixed once and for all). However, the bank MAY give a rebate for early payment at its SOLE discretion, provided there was no prior commitment to do so.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[165]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-17","front":"For which type of obligation -- Qard or Dayn -- is a penalty clause on default permitted, and why?","back":"Dayn (debt from sale/exchange transactions) -- permitted. Qard (pure loan) -- NOT permitted, since imposing a penalty on a pure loan default is usury in the strict sense; the creditor must instead give more time.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[166,167]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-18","front":"Where must penalty proceeds from a defaulting client go, and why?","back":"To CHARITY -- never to the creditor's own income -- to keep the penalty distinct from interest and from Murabaha profit.","category":"Banking","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[166]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-19","front":"What kind of 'loss' may a bank claim as liquidated damages from a defaulting client, and what is explicitly excluded?","back":"Actual, PROVEN loss (proven to the satisfaction of a court/arbitrator), determined by what the bank could have earned investing the amount elsewhere during the delay. Conventional 'opportunity cost' is explicitly excluded.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[166]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-20","front":"Define Muflis, and state the key protective rule for a genuinely insolvent debtor.","back":"Muflis = an insolvent/bankrupt debtor. If genuinely unable to pay (fraud ruled out), all his assets are sold, proceeds distributed pro rata among creditors, and he must be given time for any remaining unpaid amount.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13.1","pages":[167]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-21","front":"Define Hawalah.","back":"Literally, transfer; legally, an agreement by which a debtor is freed from a debt by another becoming responsible for it -- a contract of assignment of debt, replacing one debtor with another.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-22","front":"What is the key legal distinction between the prohibited 'sale of debt' and the permissible 'assignment of debt' (Hawalah)?","back":"Hawalah preserves RECOURSE to the original debtor if the new debtor fails to pay. A pure sale of debt has NO such recourse, which introduces Gharar/Riba, hence its prohibition.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[168]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-23","front":"Can Hawalah's effect be delayed to a future date, like Ijarah or Kafalah?","back":"No -- Hawalah must take effect immediately; it cannot be suspended, made temporary, or contingent on an unlikely future event. However, the PAYMENT DATE of the transferred debt itself can validly be deferred to a mutually agreed date.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[168]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-24","front":"Distinguish Kafalah from Rihn.","back":"Kafalah = a third party becomes surety (personally liable) for an unpaid debt. Rihn = the debtor pledges a specific asset as collateral, held by the creditor as a trust.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-25","front":"If multiple people become sureties for the same debt one after another (successively), how much is each liable for?","back":"Each successive/sequential surety is liable for the WHOLE debt (unlike simultaneous joint sureties, where each is liable only for their own share).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[169]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-26","front":"State the Hadith principle governing risk and reward in a pledge.","back":"'Pledge cannot be foreclosed, and it is from the pledger and for him is its Ghunm [gain/accession] and upon him is its Ghurm [loss]' -- the pledger (debtor) remains the owner and bears the pledge's risk and reward, not the pledgee.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15.1","pages":[170]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-27","front":"What does 'pledge cannot be foreclosed' mean in practice on default?","back":"The pledgee cannot simply appropriate/keep the pledged item on default. He must apply to the court to have the item sold and the debt recovered from the proceeds, with any excess returned to the pledger.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15.1","pages":[171]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-28","front":"Summarize the four schools' differing views on whether a pledgee may benefit from a pledge.","back":"Some Hanafis: never (even with permission -- it's Riba). Majority Hanafi: yes, with pledger's permission, if not stipulated at contract time. Shafi'e: it's the pledger's right (item stays with pledgee except when pledger uses it). Maliki: pledgee benefit OK only if the debt arose from a SALE (not Qard), stipulated at contract time, and time-bound. Hanbali: pledgee use OK with pledger's permission.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15.2","pages":[171]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-29","front":"What is the unifying principle across schools on how much benefit a pledgee may derive from a pledge?","back":"Any pledgee benefit should roughly correspond to maintenance expenditure on the pledged item (else it risks being Riba); any income beyond actual expenses should go to the pledger.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15.2","pages":[171]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-30","front":"What is the mainstream (Jamhoor Ulama) and OIC Fiqh Council position on selling debt-based Sukuk at a discount or premium (Bai' al-Dayn)?","back":"Unanimously prohibited -- even for asset-supported Sukuk debt. Traditional jurists and the overwhelming majority of contemporary scholars agree; the OIC Islamic Fiqh Council (which includes Malaysian representation) approved this prohibition unanimously, without a single dissent.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.16","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.16","pages":[172]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-31","front":"What is the actual Shafi'e-school position on Bai' al-Dayn, as clarified by Rosly and Sanusi?","back":"Shafi'e jurists allowed debt sale only AT its par (face) value -- never at a discount or premium. Some Malaysian experts miscite the Shafi'e school to justify discounted secondary trading of Sukuk, which is a misapplication.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t7.16","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.16","pages":[172]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-32","front":"Why is indexing a loan/debt to gold or a currency basket, once lent, not permitted, even though it seems to protect against inflation?","back":"Because it would let the lender draw a benefit from lending -- the debtor would 'make good' the currency's depreciation on the lender's behalf, while money the lender kept himself would simply have lost value equally. Drawing this benefit from a loan makes it non-Shari'ah-compliant.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[173,174]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch7-33","front":"Per the OIC Fiqh Council's 1993 resolution, when and how may a debt's settlement currency be changed?","back":"Only by mutual agreement made ON THE DAY OF SETTLEMENT itself (not agreed in advance), at the exchange rate prevailing on that settlement date -- a debt cannot be pre-recorded against the debtor in a fixed gold/other-currency counter-value.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[174]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e7.1","kind":"long","prompt":"Discuss the Shari’ah rules governing default by clients of Islamic banks, including penalties, compensation and insolvency.","expectedStructure":["Classical position","Why contemporary scholars allowed penalties (Ghasib analogy)","Penalty clauses and charity","Compensation: actual loss vs opportunity cost; Al Baraka Sudan approach","Qard vs Dayn distinction; OIC conditions voiding penalty","Insolvency (Muflis) and seller’s first right"],"keyConcepts":["T‘azir","liquidated damages","Muflis"],"essentialPoints":["Penalty not income","Court/arbitrator decides compensation"],"commonMistakes":["Allowing penalty as bank income","Applying penalty to Qard al Hasan"],"topicId":"t7.13","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.13","pages":[165,167]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e7.2","kind":"difference","prompt":"Differentiate between Hawalah, Kafalah and Rihn.","expectedStructure":["Definitions","Parties","Recourse and liability","Bank applications"],"keyConcepts":["assignment","surety","pledge"],"essentialPoints":["Hawalah replaces debtor","Kafalah adds surety","Rihn: debtor’s asset in trust"],"commonMistakes":["Confusing Hawalah with sale of debt"],"topicId":"t7.14","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.14","pages":[167,168]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e7.3","kind":"short","prompt":"Can an Islamic bank give a rebate on early settlement of Murabaha? Discuss.","expectedStructure":["Traditions and reconciliation","Due vs not-yet-due debts","Contemporary positions (OIC, AAOIFI)","Practical recommendation"],"keyConcepts":["Duyun Halah","Duyun Mu’ajjalah"],"essentialPoints":["Discretionary only"],"commonMistakes":["Stating rebates are prohibited in all cases"],"topicId":"t7.12","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.12","pages":[163,165]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e7.4","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Why is indexation of loans to inflation not permissible in Islamic finance?","expectedStructure":["Equality in loans","FSC and classical references","Depreciation argument","OIC rulings and alternatives"],"keyConcepts":["indexation","Riba Al-Nasiah","Riba Al-Fadl"],"essentialPoints":["Lend in gold/stable currency without increase","Settlement-day conversion"],"commonMistakes":["Suggesting inflation-linked repayment is compensation, not Riba"],"topicId":"t7.17","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.17","pages":[172,174]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e7.5","kind":"viva","prompt":"Who bears the loss if a pledged car is destroyed in an accident without the bank’s fault?","expectedStructure":["Pledger’s ownership","Ghunm/Ghurm Hadith"],"keyConcepts":["Rihn"],"essentialPoints":["Bank still recovers the loan"],"commonMistakes":["Placing the loss on the pledgee"],"topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e7.6","kind":"long","prompt":"Explain the Shari’ah rules on securing debts through Kafalah and Rihn, including risk, benefit and enforcement of a pledge.","expectedStructure":["Textual basis (2:283; the Prophet’s breastplate)","Kafalah: third-party surety; joint and successive sureties","Rihn: pledge as trust; risk with pledger (Ghunm/Ghurm)","No foreclosure; sale through court or power of attorney","Benefit from pledge: views of the four schools","Bank practice: securities, lien, mortgage"],"keyConcepts":["Kafalah","Rihn","Ghunm bil Ghurm","possessory lien","Hamish Jiddiyah"],"essentialPoints":["Pledge covers only the debt","Excess returned to pledger","Bank may benefit for maintenance; extra to pledger"],"commonMistakes":["Letting the bank keep the pledge in lieu of debt","Placing the loss of a pledge on the bank without negligence"],"topicId":"t7.15","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.15","pages":[168,172]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-23","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the Shari'ah treatment of loans (Qard): why no increment over the principal is allowed, and how this differs from a legitimate practice of voluntarily paying back more than the principal.","expectedStructure":["State the basic rule: only the principal is repayable; any stipulated excess is Riba","Explain that this applies regardless of who the parties are (Muslim/non-Muslim, employer/employee, State/individual)","Describe Husnal Qadha (gracious repayment) as a legitimate but strictly voluntary, non-systematic practice","Explain why turning gracious repayment into a system (explicit or implicit) would itself amount to Riba"],"keyConcepts":["Qard","Riba","Husnal Qadha","Time value of money (loans vs trade)"],"essentialPoints":["A loan (Qard) must be repaid in the same amount only; any loan whereby something in excess of the principal is exacted, stipulated or expected becomes unlawful as Riba, with no exemption based on who the parties are.","Unlike in trade, where a credit price can legitimately differ from a cash price, no value can be assigned to the time given for payment of a loan or debt (Dayn) once its amount is fixed, because Qard and Dayn are treated as virtuous/benevolent acts from which the lender may not benefit.","Husnal Qadha — voluntarily repaying more than the principal without any precondition, as the Prophet (pbuh) himself did — is commendable and Sunnah-compatible, but remains a matter of individual discretion rather than a system.","If gracious repayment were adopted systematically by banks or the State (explicit, as a customary rate, or implicit, as an expected return), it would effectively convert the loan into an interest-bearing instrument, which the book rejects; this is why current account deposits (treated as Qard to the bank) are guaranteed only at face value and carry no contractual return."],"commonMistakes":["Assuming Husnal Qadha legitimizes banks routinely paying a 'bonus' on deposits — the book explicitly states that making this a system implies a stipulated addition, which is Riba.","Conflating the permissible time-value pricing of goods/usufruct in trade with the prohibited claim to the time value of a loan or debt itself."],"topicId":"t7.11","chapter":7,"source":{"chapter":7,"section":"7.11","pages":[159,162,163]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
